      <div class="tldr" role="note" aria-label="Summary in three sentences">
        <p class="tldr__label">In three sentences</p>
        <ol>
          <li>When you exercise discretionary influence over others' conditions and they cannot exit or contest it, you owe them an account.</li>
          <li>The account must take at least one of three forms: meaningful exit, independent review, or justification open to contestation. J is necessary but not sufficient: it must be paired with the Floor and assessed against SY1–SY3.</li>
          <li>You cannot be the sole judge of whether the account is good enough — the more powerful party bears a heightened burden to seek independent evidence and correction.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <details class="cheatsheet">
        <summary>Quick reference (one screen)</summary>
        <div class="cheatsheet__grid">
          <div><h4>Universal vs normative symbiosis</h4><p>Universal: existence is relationally woven (descriptive). Normative: SY1–SY3 floor (prescriptive). The second does not follow from the first (§2.0a, §5).</p></div>
          <div><h4>Principle J</h4><p>Systematic constraint → at least one of: meaningful exit, independent review, justification open to contestation. Necessary but not sufficient.</p></div>
          <div><h4>Activation rule</h4><p>Gate: discretion. Drivers: E, S, C. L1 always; L2 if one High or two Moderate; L3 if S High + (E or C) High.</p></div>
          <div><h4>Duty ladder</h4><p>L1 explain · L2 review &amp; respond · L3 preserve, examine, authorise.</p></div>
          <div><h4>Floor (F1–F5)</h4><p>Exit or voice · contestation · development · non-foreclosure · recognition.</p></div>
          <div><h4>Symbiosis (SY1–SY3)</h4><p>Non-exploitation · non-assimilation · continued possibility. Structural indicators (iii)/(iv) carry more confirmatory weight than SY1.</p></div>
          <div><h4>Three kinds of claim</h4><p>Logical consequences · normative commitments · practical recommendations (§2.0a).</p></div>
          <div><h4>Apophatic constraint</h4><p>You cannot certify that you have avoided imposing your understanding. You can make the imposition nameable, revisable and resistible (A1–A4). In the Limit 3 case, A1–A4 remain obligations but cannot be enforced.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <section id="abstract">
        <div class="abstract">
          <h2 class="abstract__title">Abstract</h2>
          <p class="abstract__lede"><strong>In one sentence:</strong> when you exercise discretionary influence over the conditions under which others live, choose and develop, and when they cannot exit or contest that influence effectively, you owe them an <abbr title="The duty to give reasons, permit examination, and remain open to correction.">account</abbr> of it — and you cannot be the sole judge of whether that account is good enough.</p>
          <p><strong>What this essay is, and what it is not.</strong> It is a framework for deciding when influence creates a duty to answer, how demanding that duty is, who must discharge it, and how it can be discharged without turning into new <abbr title="The standing capacity to interfere arbitrarily, without being forced to track the interests of affected.">domination</abbr>. It is not a new foundational principle in the tradition of Pettit, Forst or Jonas. Its core principle (J) is a <em>thin, disjunctive, abductively supported</em> synthesis of existing traditions, not a demonstration of necessity. <strong>J is necessary but not sufficient</strong>: it must be paired with the Floor (<a href="#s10">§10</a>) and assessed against SY1–SY3 (<a href="#s5">§5.1</a>), and a relation can satisfy J genuinely and fail substantively (<a href="#s2-1">§2.1</a>). Its contribution is stated openly in <a href="#s1-5">§1.5</a> and shown in Table 1: an interlocking operational set (activation rule, duty ladder, T5-precondition, epistemic burden principle, allocation by exercised capacity) that no single tradition supplies. Its normative location is stated openly in <a href="#s2-7">§2.7</a>: internal to the justificatory tradition, but only to its <em>thin</em> version.</p>
          <p><strong>Universal symbiosis and normative symbiosis.</strong> The essay rests on a descriptive worldview — <em>universal symbiosis</em> — that existence is relationally woven: dependence, influence, interaction, competition, conflict, destruction and mutual creation are all part of the fabric. It distinguishes this sharply from <em>normative symbiosis</em>: the pro tanto floor specified by SY1–SY3, which says that certain ways of arranging relations are unacceptable. The second does not follow from the first (<a href="#s2-0a">§2.0a</a>). The framework states its premises openly (<a href="#s2-0a">§2.0a</a>), distinguishes logical consequences from normative commitments from practical recommendations, and argues rather than derives its normative core.</p>
          <p><strong>1. Ground.</strong> J is a thin, disjunctive principle: arrangements that systematically constrain others' conditions must provide at least one of meaningful exit, independent review, or justification open to contestation. Which is required depends on the structure of the constraint. J is primarily an epistemic principle: it protects against the actor confusing their own view with the truth. J is <em>abductively</em> supported and has stated defeaters (<a href="#s2">§2.2b</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>2. Standing and symbiosis.</strong> Who is owed an account, including beings that cannot speak for themselves, handled through four tiers of standing under uncertainty with an explicit plausibility threshold (<a href="#s4">§4</a>). Symbiosis is specified with observable indicators (SY1–SY3), so that the framework's floor is as contestable as its procedure (<a href="#s5">§5.1</a>). SY1 is the weakest of the four sources of confirmation in §15.2b: where preferences are adaptive, stated preferences and accepted terms may converge for the wrong reason, and structural indicators (iii) and (iv) should be given more weight. Section <a href="#s5">§5.2</a> develops a more limited claim: asymmetric influence creates a heightened burden to seek correction from sources not controlled by the decision-maker.</p>
          <p><strong>3. Activation.</strong> One rule: a discretion gate plus three drivers (entrenchment, severity, contestation deficit) that determine one of three levels of duty, with anchors and a rating protocol (<a href="#s6">§6</a>–<a href="#s9">9</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>4. Structure.</strong> A floor of minimum conditions, the prohibition on being sole judge of one's own influence, the reviewer regress treated as a design problem, and accounts of conflict, future generations, ecological wholes, genuine tragedy, and learning (<a href="#s10">§10</a>–<a href="#s13">13</a>, <a href="#s12-6">§12.6</a>, <a href="#s12-7">§12.7</a>).</p>
          <p><strong>5. Application and testing.</strong> Worked cases including one that appears to refute the framework (<a href="#s14">§14.2b</a>) and one that shows the T5-precondition failing (<a href="#s14-7">§14.7</a>), five proposed evaluation conditions for the activation rule, and modular conditions for replacement (<a href="#s15">§15.5</a>). The empirical tests are proposed, not performed.</p>
          <p><strong>Answerability is the procedure. Normative symbiosis is the floor.</strong> The framework does not claim that a relation meeting SY1–SY3 is therefore good. It claims that a relation failing SY1–SY3 is not good, whatever else it is.</p>
          <p><strong>The apophatic constraint</strong> (<a href="#s15">§15.2b</a>) is what follows from the framework's hardest question: the superior cannot certify that it has avoided imposing its own understanding, but it can preserve the structural conditions under which the being can express a self-understanding the superior cannot anticipate — provided a party outside the superior can verify compliance. In the Limit 3 case, where no such party exists, A1–A4 cannot be enforced, though they remain obligations. A1–A4 are stated as <em>normative commitments</em>, not logical consequences of the framework's premises (<a href="#s2-0a">§2.0a</a>).</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="preface" id="preface" aria-labelledby="preface-h">
        <h2 id="preface-h">The argument in brief</h2>
        <p>The essay is long, and its spine can be stated in a page. This is that page.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>The worldview.</strong> Existence is relationally woven — <em>universal symbiosis</em>. The relations include dependence, influence, interaction, competition, conflict, destruction and mutual creation. This is a descriptive claim about the structure of reality, not an ethics (<a href="#s5">§5</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The problem.</strong> Influence that is structural, persistent, diffuse and hard to attribute to any single wrongful act is not adequately covered by existing moral vocabulary — blame, rights, consent, regulation. This is the gap the framework addresses (<a href="#s1">§1</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The principle (J).</strong> Arrangements that systematically and non-trivially constrain others' conditions must, for those affected, provide at least one of: meaningful exit, independent review, or justification open to contestation. Where exit and review work, justification need not be owed; where they fail, it is (<a href="#s2">§2.1</a>). J is <em>necessary but not sufficient</em>: it must be paired with the Floor and assessed against SY1–SY3 (§2.1).</li>
          <li><strong>The normative step.</strong> The move from universal symbiosis to normative symbiosis does not follow from the relational premise alone. It requires additional premises — standing, vulnerability, capacity, fallibility, contestation — and the framework states them openly and distinguishes logical consequences from normative commitments from practical recommendations (<a href="#s2-0a">§2.0a</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Why J.</strong> J is <em>abductively</em> supported. Four traditions — justificatory, republican, epistemic, relational — make J plausible, but they are treated as <em>considerations</em>, not as independent proofs. J has stated defeaters (<a href="#s2">§2.2b</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The trigger.</strong> Influence creates the duty when discretion is present and exit or review is unavailable or ineffective. How much duty scales with entrenchment, severity and contestation deficit (<a href="#s7">§7</a>–<a href="#s8">§8</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The epistemic claim.</strong> Under asymmetric influence, the more powerful party has weaker grounds for treating its own assessment as decisive. It therefore bears a heightened burden to obtain independent evidence and, where possible, correction from those affected. This is a defeasible claim, not a necessary condition for knowledge (<a href="#s5">§5.2</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>The limits.</strong> J is not sufficient. It is defensible. Its activation rule has proposed evaluation conditions; its ground and epistemic principle have stated defeaters. None of these is a proof of anything. They are invitations to argue.</li>
          <li><strong>The hardest question.</strong> Can a superior intelligence preserve a being's existence, freedom and development without understanding what the being is — and without making its own understanding the measure? The framework's answer (<a href="#s15">§15.2b</a>) is that it cannot certify that it has avoided making its own understanding the measure, but it can preserve the conditions under which the imposition is nameable, revisable and resistible. Four structural conditions follow: preserve the capacity to resist (A1), do not require justification in the superior's terms (A2), do not interpret non-resistance as consent (A3), treat resistance as evidence against the superior's understanding (A4). These conditions are not self-enforcing, and in the Limit 3 case — where no party outside the superior can verify compliance — they cannot be enforced, though they remain obligations (<a href="#s15">§15.2b</a>).</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>What it is not.</strong> The framework is not a procedure for certifying legitimacy. A relation can follow every rule and still fail to be legitimate. The framework's job is to name that possibility, not to deny it.</p>
        <p class="preface-note">If you read only one section after this page, read <a href="#s7">§7</a> — that is where the framework either works or does not. If you read only one passage, read <a href="#s5">§5.2</a> — that is where it makes its main epistemic proposal. If you want to know what the framework cannot do, read <a href="#s15">§15.2</a>, <a href="#s15">§15.2b</a> and <a href="#s15-6">§15.6</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="reader-guide" id="reader-guide" aria-labelledby="reader-guide-h">
        <h2 id="reader-guide-h">How to read this essay</h2>
        <p>This essay has eighteen numbered sections in five parts, plus supplementary subsections (1.5, 2.0a, 2.2b, 2.7, 2.8, 3.3, 3.4, 5.0a, 5.1, 5.2, 5.3, 5.4, 12.0, 12.6, 12.7, 14.1b, 14.2b, 14.7, 14.7b, 15.2b, 15.6, 15.7) that carry some of its more substantive claims. Several of these are load-bearing. It is dense by design, but it does not have to be read linearly.</p>

        <h3>The argument at a glance</h3>
        <div class="roadmap-grid">
          <div class="roadmap-card">
            <p class="roadmap-card__label">Part I</p>
            <p class="roadmap-card__title">Problem, Scope and Ground</p>
