        <h3>Five questions the framework addresses</h3>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>What is universal symbiosis, and does it entail normative symbiosis?</strong> <a href="#s5">§5</a> and <a href="#s2-0a">§2.0a</a>. The first is descriptive; the second is normative; the move between them requires stated additional premises.</li>
          <li><strong>When does influence create duty?</strong> <a href="#s7">§7</a> gives the activation rule — a discretion gate plus three drivers.</li>
          <li><strong>How much duty?</strong> <a href="#s8">§8</a> gives a three-level ladder from explanation, through review, to independent authorisation.</li>
          <li><strong>Whose interests count?</strong> <a href="#s4">§4</a> gives four tiers of standing under uncertainty; <a href="#s10">§10</a> gives five minimum conditions in relationships of dependence.</li>
          <li><strong>Who watches the powerful?</strong> No actor should be the sole judge of its own influence (<a href="#s11">§11</a>). <a href="#s11">§11.4</a> converts the regress into a design constraint.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>What the framework does not do</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>It does not tell you what to decide. It tells you what questions you owe an answer to — and to whom.</li>
          <li>It does not claim neutrality. It takes a substantive moral position (<a href="#s2">§2</a>) and states its normative location openly (<a href="#s2-7">§2.7</a>).</li>
          <li>It does not eliminate the problem of power (<a href="#s11">§11.4</a>, <a href="#s2">§2.4b</a>). It converts the regress into a design constraint — and it says so when the conversion fails.</li>
          <li>It does not claim universality for J. It claims that four families of consideration point toward it (<a href="#s2">§2.3</a>), and that this is evidence but not proof.</li>
          <li>It does not claim that J is sufficient. It claims that J is <em>necessary but not sufficient</em>, and that it has stated defeaters.</li>
          <li>It does not claim that normative symbiosis is a complete ideal. It claims that it is a pro tanto floor derived from the thin standing claim (<a href="#s5-3">§5.3</a>).</li>
          <li>It does not solve domination. It handles it (<a href="#s3">§3.2</a>, <a href="#s16">§16</a>).</li>
          <li>It is a diagnostic, not a procedure.</li>
          <li>It does not claim that a superior intelligence can act toward a being it does not understand without imposing its own categories (<a href="#s15">§15.2b</a>). It claims that such an intelligence can make the imposition nameable, revisable and resistible — and that A1–A4 are normative commitments, not logical consequences of the framework's premises.</li>
          <li>It does not claim that A1–A4 are self-enforcing. Five named failure modes specify how they can be satisfied in form and violated in substance. In the Limit 3 case, they cannot be enforced, though they remain obligations (<a href="#s15">§15.2b</a>).</li>
          <li>It does not claim that its empirical tests have been performed. They are proposed, not carried out (<a href="#s15">§15.4</a>, <a href="#appendix-a">Appendix A</a>).</li>
        </ul>

