      <div class="part-divider" id="part-i">
        <p class="part-divider__label">Part I</p>
        <h2 class="part-divider__title">Problem, Scope and Ground</h2>
      </div>

      <section id="s1">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">1.</span>The Problem, Scope and Terms</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">The capacity to shape other people's conditions has grown faster than the practices for answering for it.</p>

        <p>Recommendation systems, credit scoring, clinical triage tools and AI assistants shape the conditions of millions. So do states writing policy and companies setting terms, and, at a different scale, clinicians, teachers, landlords and parents. Existing moral vocabulary covers parts of this territory: blame for wrongful acts, rights for protected interests, consent for voluntary exchanges, regulation for what law has already caught. What falls between them is influence that is structural, persistent, diffuse and hard to attribute to any single wrongful act, and that nonetheless determines what others can do and become.</p>

        <h4 id="s1-1">1.1 What the framework does and does not do</h4>
        <p>It answers four questions: when does influence create a duty to give an account; how demanding is that duty; who must discharge it; and how can review be organised so that it does not become a new instrument of domination. It does not tell anyone what to decide. It is not a decision theory, and it is not a substitute for law, rights or substantive standards of justice.</p>

        <h4 id="s1-2">1.2 Scope conditions: when the framework does not apply</h4>
        <p>The framework is offered for a specific class of cases. It does not claim to apply everywhere.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Symmetrical relations.</strong> Where parties have roughly equal capacity to exit, review and contest, J is trivially satisfied or not engaged.</li>
          <li><strong>Relations governed by enforceable rights and effective regulation.</strong> Where law provides effective rights, review and remedies, the framework may add little. Formal legality alone is not enough: rights may be inaccessible, remedies delayed, or review structurally dependent on the actor. The framework supplements legal standards where those protections fail in practice; it never displaces them.</li>
          <li><strong>Purely causal influence without discretion.</strong> A person who is swept away by a flood does not owe an account to those downstream. The framework's trigger is <em>exercised or maintained discretionary influence</em>, not mere causal connection.</li>
          <li><strong>Trivial influence.</strong> A café changing its menu is not the case the framework addresses. The severity floor in <a href="#s7">§7</a> is meant to exclude it.</li>
          <li><strong>Relations where the affected party is not a candidate for standing.</strong> Stones and rivers as such do not have standing under this framework (<a href="#s4">§4.1</a>).</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="s1-3">1.3 Terms</h4>
        <p><strong>Influence:</strong> the exercise or maintenance of control over conditions under which others act, choose, develop or fare. Maintaining a structure one could alter counts as influence. Mere ability to help does not (<a href="#s3">§3</a>).</p>
        <p><strong>Actor:</strong> a person, organisation or institution, including those that design, deploy and maintain technical systems. Technical systems are treated as instruments, and answerability falls on the humans and organisations behind them.</p>
        <p><strong>Affected party:</strong> any being whose interests are materially affected by the influence.</p>
        <p><strong>Discretion:</strong> the actor has genuine alternatives at tolerable cost and a role in setting the influence, rather than merely executing a rule it had no part in making or contesting.</p>
        <p><strong>Answerability:</strong> a duty to give reasons for the conditions one helps create or maintain, to those affected or their representatives; to permit examination of those reasons; and to remain open to correction. It is distinct from <em>blame</em>, <em>liability</em> and <em>authority</em>.</p>

        <p><strong>Five terms that must be kept apart.</strong> The framework uses five terms that ordinary language often conflates. Each names a different thing, and the framework's claims depend on not mixing them:</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Table 1a. Answerability, responsibility, blame, authority, sovereignty</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Term</th>
                <th scope="col">What it names</th>
                <th scope="col">What it does not entail</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Answerability</strong></td>
                <td>The duty to give reasons, permit examination, and remain open to correction (§5.2)</td>
                <td>It does not entail blame (a party can be answerable without being at fault), liability (a party can be answerable without bearing the cost), or authority (a party can be answerable without having the right to decide).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Responsibility</strong></td>
                <td>Being the party whose actions, omissions, or maintained structures make a difference to what happens</td>
                <td>It does not entail blame (causal responsibility can exist without fault), or authority (being responsible for an outcome does not give one the right to control it).</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Blame</strong></td>
                <td>A moral judgment that a party is at fault for a wrongful act or omission</td>
                <td>It does not follow from answerability alone. A party can be answerable for an outcome it did not cause wrongfully, and can be blamed for an outcome it is not answerable for.</td>
              </tr>
