        <p><strong>The key implication.</strong> A party can bear a large answerability burden — can be required to explain, to submit to review, to justify — without being morally at fault for what happened, and without acquiring any authority over those it is answerable to. The framework's central claim is that these five things come apart, and that treating them as one thing is what produces the confusion the framework is meant to address.</p>

        <h4 id="s1-4">1.4 Relation to existing work</h4>
        <p>The framework is a synthesis and an operationalisation, and its intellectual debts are substantial: Pettit and Lovett on domination, Forst on the right to justification, Scanlon on reasonable rejection, Young on structural responsibility, Bovens and Shoemaker on accountability and answerability, Tronto, Held and Goodin on care and vulnerability. <strong>Its contribution is comparative and operational, with a limited philosophical proposal rather than a new foundational principle.</strong></p>

        <h4 id="s1-5">1.5 The specific claim, in one paragraph</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">If the framework has a single contribution, it should be statable in a paragraph — and then shown.</p>

        <p>The framework's contribution is not that non-domination matters (Pettit), that those subject to norms have a right to justification (Forst), that responsibility for structural harm is shared (Young), or that capability creates responsibility for the future (Jonas). All of that is in the tradition. The framework's contribution is the following claim:</p>

        <div class="key-prop" data-label="The specific claim">
          <p><strong>The evidential burden of justification under asymmetric influence is not adequately captured by any of the existing traditions, because none of them specifies how the burden is allocated, how it is graduated by entrenchment, severity and contestation deficit, or how review mechanisms can be designed so that they degrade rather than merely consult the powerful actor's claimed legitimacy.</strong> The framework's contribution is to specify (i) the activation rule, (ii) the duty ladder, (iii) the T5-precondition, and (iv) the epistemic burden principle, as an interlocking set of operational proposals that no single tradition supplies.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Why this is not just a synthesis.</strong> A synthesis of existing positions would be a combination. The framework is not a combination. It is an <em>interlocking set</em> — each piece depends on the others, and the whole does work that none of the parts does.</p>

        <p><strong>Adjacent literatures the table does not name.</strong> Several operational components have close analogues in literatures not conventionally grouped with the four traditions in Table 1. <em>Accountable governance</em> (Bovens 2007; Mulgan 2003; Romzek &amp; Dubnick 1987) supplies the concept of answerability and the distinction between answerability and accountability. <em>Risk-based regulation</em> (Black 2010; Hood, Rothstein &amp; Baldwin 2001; Hutter 2005) supplies the graduated, risk-proportional structure that is the closest institutional analog of the duty ladder. <em>Administrative-law proportionality</em> (Alexy 2002; Cohen-Eliya &amp; Porat 2011; Mashaw 1983) supplies the logical form of the level distinctions. <em>Experimentalist governance</em> (Sabel &amp; Zeitlin 2010) supplies the iterative review-and-correction structure that the T5-precondition formalises. <em>The precaution / cost-benefit debate</em> (Sunstein 2005; Wiener 2016) supplies the threshold debate that §4.2 enters.</p>

        <p><strong>What the traditions supply, and what this framework operationalises.</strong> The table below states, for each component, whether the component is present in the four traditions the framework engages, or operationalised here.</p>

