        <p><strong>What the duty ladder adds to risk-based regulation.</strong> Risk-based regulation (Black 2010; Hood, Rothstein &amp; Baldwin 2001) already graduates obligations by risk. The duty ladder adds three things that risk-based regulation does not supply. First, the ladder specifies <em>what kind</em> of obligation attaches at each level — explanation, review and response, or preservation and independent authorisation — not merely how much. Second, the ladder is tied to the T5-precondition: Level 3 authorisation is not valid without prior publication of the authorising body's R1 indicators. Third, the ladder is allocated by capacity (§8.2), not by risk alone.</p>

