      <section id="s2">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">2.</span>The Ground and Its Support</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">The framework needs a thin, defensible principle, an abductive justification that is honest about its own weakness, and stated defeaters for the ground itself.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-0">2.0 A motivating premise: fallibilism about understanding</h4>
        <div class="key-prop" data-label="Premise (F) and bridge norm (B)">
          <p><strong>F (fallibilism).</strong> No actor fully understands the conditions it shapes, or everything that depends on what it does. This is a claim about the limits of understanding, not about the cosmos and not about any ranking of beings.</p>
          <p><strong>B (bridge norm).</strong> Where an actor's influence falls on others who bear the cost of its errors, and who may see what the actor cannot, the actor should not be the sole judge of that influence; it should seek correction from them.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>What F and B do.</strong> F alone yields no duty. A fact about limited understanding does not by itself entail a norm, which is why B is stated openly as a normative premise rather than smuggled in. Together, F and B <em>motivate</em> J (§2.1) and the epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5">§5.2</a>). They do not <em>entail</em> J.</p>

        <p><strong>Why an obligation, and not merely prudence or benevolence.</strong> Prudence would say: it is in the actor's interest to seek correction. Benevolence would say: it is kind of the actor to seek correction. B says neither. It says that the affected party has a <em>claim</em> on the actor — a claim grounded in the fact that the actor's errors fall on them and that they may see what the actor cannot. A claim is not a recommendation. It is something the affected party can press, and something the actor can fail to meet.</p>

        <p><strong>What depends on F, and what does not.</strong> The motivation for J and for the epistemic burden principle draws on F and B. The exercised-capacity principle (<a href="#s3">§3</a>) does not. The activation rule, duty ladder, rating protocol, floor and the T5 precondition are procedural and stand or fall on their own tests (<a href="#s15">§15.4</a>). The apophatic constraint and A1–A4 (§15.2b) are conditional on F. A reader who rejects F can therefore still use the framework's procedural machinery, but that reader loses A1–A4 and the apophatic constraint, and therefore loses the answer to the hardest question in §15.2b. This is a real loss, not merely a loss of motivation. The full dependency map is in §15.1 (Table 7).</p>

        <p><strong>What would refute F as motivation.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>FD1.</strong> There is an actor or domain whose understanding of the conditions it shapes is in fact complete.</li>
          <li><strong>FD2.</strong> The incompleteness is contingent and the actor alone can reliably remedy it.</li>
          <li><strong>FD3.</strong> Correction by those affected does not, on balance, improve the actor's account or outcomes.</li>
          <li><strong>FD4.</strong> J and §5.2 are as well supported without F as with it.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="s2-0a">2.0a What the relational premise does not do</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">The claim that existence is relationally woven does not by itself entail any normative claim. This section states the additional premises explicitly, and distinguishes three kinds of claim.</p>

        <p>The claim that existence is relationally woven (universal symbiosis, §5) does not by itself entail any normative claim. From 'A exists in relation to B' nothing follows about how A should treat B, any more than from 'A is spatially near B' anything follows about how A should treat B. The relational premise is a claim about the structure of reality. A normative claim is a claim about what is owed. The two are different kinds of claim, and the essay does not derive the second from the first.</p>

        <p><strong>The additional premises.</strong> What the essay does instead is state the additional premises that, together with the relational premise, motivate the framework's normative claims:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>P1 (standing).</strong> Affected parties have moral weight that cannot be reduced to their instrumental value to the powerful. (§2.7, thin standing.)</li>
          <li><strong>P2 (vulnerability).</strong> Affected parties can be harmed by the exercise of discretionary influence over their conditions. (§4.)</li>
          <li><strong>P3 (capacity).</strong> Some actors have the capacity to exercise such influence, and some do not. (§3.)</li>
          <li><strong>P4 (fallibility).</strong> No actor fully understands the conditions it shapes. (F, §2.0.)</li>
          <li><strong>P5 (contestation).</strong> Affected parties have a claim that the actor not be the sole judge of the influence when they bear the cost of its errors and may see what the actor cannot. (B, §2.0.)</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The framework's normative claims — J (§2.1), the activation rule (§7), the duty ladder (§8), SY1–SY3 (§5.1) — follow from these premises together. They do not follow from any one premise alone, and they do not follow from the relational premise alone.</p>

        <p><strong>Three kinds of claim, kept apart.</strong> The essay distinguishes three kinds of claim, and readers should be able to tell which is which:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Logical consequences.</strong> If P1–P5 are accepted, some conclusions follow deductively. For example: if B is accepted, then an actor that controls both the influence and the evidence used to assess it cannot close the question using only evidence it controls. This is a consequence, not a separate commitment.</li>
          <li><strong>Normative commitments.</strong> Some claims are not deduced from the premises but are normative commitments the framework states openly: J, the floor (F1–F5), SY1–SY3, and A1–A4. These are defended by argument (§2.3, §5.3, §15.2b) but they are not theorems. A reader who accepts P1–P5 but rejects J has not made a logical error; they have made a different normative choice. The framework argues for J but does not claim to compel acceptance.</li>
          <li><strong>Practical recommendations.</strong> The activation rule (§7), the duty ladder (§8), the rating protocol (§9), the T5-precondition (§11.4), the pilot protocol (Appendix A) are practical recommendations. They are offered as the best available way to operationalise the framework's normative commitments, but they stand or fall on their own tests (§15.4). A reader who accepts J but rejects the specific thresholds in §9.1 has accepted the framework's ethics and rejected its implementation.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Why this matters.</strong> The three-way distinction is the essay's answer to the charge that it commits an is–ought fallacy. It does not derive norms from facts. It states its premises, argues for its normative commitments, and offers its practical recommendations as testable proposals. A reader who disagrees can say precisely where: at the premises (§2.7), at the normative commitments (§2.3, §5.3, §15.2b), or at the practical recommendations (§15.4).</p>

        <h4 id="s2-1">2.1 The core principle (J)</h4>
        <div class="key-prop" data-label="The core principle (J)">
          <p>Any arrangement that systematically and non-trivially constrains the conditions under which others act, choose or develop must, for those affected, provide at least one of three things: <strong>meaningful exit</strong>, <strong>independent review</strong>, or <strong>justification that is genuinely open to their contestation</strong>. Where exit is available at tolerable cost and review is effective, justification need not be owed. Where they are not, justification is required, and the strength of the requirement scales with how thoroughly exit and review fail.</p>
        </div>

        <p>J is deliberately thin. It does not say what is good, which outcomes are required, or who prevails when interests conflict. It says what kind of relationship must exist between those who shape conditions and those who live in them: one in which the affected are not simply subject to unaccountable power.</p>

        <p><strong>What the qualifiers require.</strong> J's three disjuncts are not satisfied by the bare existence of exit, review, or justification. Each has a minimum threshold, specified elsewhere in the framework and collected here:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Meaningful exit</strong> requires that the affected party can leave at tolerable cost without losing the conditions for continued existence, development, or participation (F1, F3, F4, §10) — and that the cost of leaving is not itself a mechanism of the influence being escaped.</li>
          <li><strong>Independent review</strong> requires that the reviewer meets R1–R6 (§11.3).</li>
          <li><strong>Justification genuinely open to contestation</strong> requires that dissent channels exist and have been used, that the vocabulary for objection is available, and that intermediaries not controlled by the actor exist (§15.2b, sources (iii) and (iv)). A published statement that no one can contest is not a justification in this sense.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A power structure can satisfy the <em>form</em> of any of these — a formal exit route, a formal review body, a formal statement of reasons — and fail the <em>substance</em>. When it does, J is not satisfied.</p>

        <p><strong>When none of the three is practically possible.</strong> There are cases where exit is unavailable, review cannot reach the actor, and contestation is not open to the affected party. In such cases J is not satisfied, and the framework does not claim that it can be. What the framework can do is name the failure, identify which of the three disjuncts is missing and why, and — where possible — specify what would be required to restore one of them. What it cannot do is supply a fourth mechanism that works where all three fail.</p>

        <p><strong>J is primarily an epistemic principle.</strong> J is not a claim that justification <em>makes</em> actions right. It is a claim that actors who cannot be contested — whether by exit, review or justification — are more likely to be wrong, and that the practice of contestation is the best available protection against this.</p>

        <p><strong>J is defensible, not necessary as a deduction.</strong> The framework does not claim that J is a necessary truth. It claims that J is <em>abductively defensible</em>: that it explains important recurring risks of unchecked discretion while remaining compatible with plural substantive theories of justice.</p>

        <h5>J is necessary but not sufficient for justice</h5>

        <p><strong>The objection.</strong> A disjunctive principle can be satisfied in more than one form. Can a power structure genuinely meet J and the Floor and still be deeply unjust? Yes. The framework says so, and this section says what follows.</p>

        <p><strong>The case.</strong> Consider a society that genuinely satisfies J and the Floor. Exit is available at tolerable cost; review is independent and meets R1–R6; justification is open to contestation, and the contestation is effective. F1–F5 hold. The society is not colonial, not captured, and not in bad faith. Yet it is deeply unjust, because the distribution of resources is such that one group, while formally free to exit and contest, cannot in practice use either: the cost of exit is tolerable in principle but not for them, and the contestation channels require resources they do not have. The society satisfies J and the Floor, and it is unjust. What it fails is not J or the Floor but something the framework does not measure: the material conditions under which formal freedoms are usable.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework says.</strong> This case does not refute J; it shows what J was never meant to do. J specifies the relationship between those who shape conditions and those who live in them. It does not specify the distribution of resources, opportunities or capabilities. A society can satisfy J and the Floor and still fail on distributive justice. The framework's response is not to expand J to cover distribution — that would make it a substantive theory of justice, which it is not — but to say that J and the Floor are necessary conditions for a non-arbitrary relation, not sufficient conditions for a just one. SY1–SY3 catch some distributive failures (SY1 catches exploitative terms, SY3 catches foreclosed development), but they do not catch all of them.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>J can be genuinely satisfied and the relation still be unjust, because J is procedural and relational, not distributive.</li>
            <li>The qualifiers — meaningful exit, independent review, genuinely contestable justification — are load-bearing, but they do not make J sufficient.</li>
            <li>J must be paired with the Floor (F1–F5) and assessed against SY1–SY3; J alone is not sufficient.</li>
            <li>J is a necessary condition for a non-arbitrary relation, not a sufficient one.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h4 id="s2-2b">2.2b What would refute J: stated defeaters</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">A principle that cannot be defeated is not a principle; it is a dogma.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>D1 — The unaccountable-power hypothesis is false.</strong> If it can be shown empirically that actors with unaccountable discretion over others' conditions do <em>not</em> systematically produce worse outcomes — measured independently of the actors' own reports — than actors subject to exit, review or contestation, then J's epistemic justification collapses.</li>
          <li><strong>D2 — A better principle explains the same cases.</strong> If a rival principle explains all the cases J explains, does so more simply, and generates clearer practical guidance, then J should be replaced.</li>
          <li><strong>D3 — The convergence is an artefact.</strong> If it can be shown that the four "routes" to J (<a href="#s2">§2.3</a>) are not independent considerations but one tradition dressed in four vocabularies, then the argument for J reduces to a single route.</li>
          <li><strong>D4 — The costs exceed the benefits.</strong> If adopting J produces worse outcomes for the affected than not adopting it — measured by independent indicators — then J should be abandoned.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>These defeaters are stated in advance so that the framework can be corrected. The operational form of D1–D4 is in <a href="#s15-7">§15.7 (Table 8)</a>.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-3">2.3 Why J: an abductive justification</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">J is offered as a plausible explanation of two recurring risks of unchecked discretionary power: domination and epistemic error.</p>

        <p>The framework does not rest on a single theory. It rests on an <strong>abductive inference</strong>: J is proposed as a compact explanation of a set of recurring risks associated with unaccountable power.</p>

        <p><strong>Four considerations, not four independent proofs.</strong> The framework draws on four families of consideration that point toward J:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The justificatory consideration.</strong> If principles governing relations among agents must be justifiable to each of them (Scanlon 1998; Forst 2012), then constraint that cannot be justified to the constrained treats them as objects of management rather than as participants.</li>
          <li><strong>The republican consideration.</strong> The distinctive wrong of domination is the standing capacity to interfere arbitrarily (Pettit 1997, 2012; Lovett 2010). Contestability is the remedy.</li>
          <li><strong>The epistemic consideration.</strong> Actors are fallible, partial about their own influence, and prone to self-serving judgment. Requiring them to explain and to expose their reasons to challenge improves decisions by almost any standard of what is good. This is the <em>primary</em> ground of J.</li>
          <li><strong>The relational consideration.</strong> Obligations arise within dependence. Those with power over dependents carry special responsibilities, and justification must attend to the particular situation of the vulnerable (Tronto 1993; Held 2006; Goodin 1985).</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The convergence of four considerations is support, not proof. The considerations are not fully independent, and the epistemic consideration is primary.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>J is <em>abductively</em> supported, without claiming to be the only or uniquely best explanation.</li>
            <li>Four families of consideration point toward J. They are not fully independent. The epistemic consideration is primary.</li>
            <li>The convergence is offered as support, not as proof.</li>
            <li>The defeaters for J are stated in advance (<a href="#s2">§2.2b</a>).</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h4 id="s2-4">2.4 Rivals, taken in their best forms</h4>
        <p><strong>Consequentialism.</strong> Sophisticated consequentialists can accept J as an institutional implication, especially in its disjunctive form.</p>
        <p><strong>Kantianism.</strong> It shares the core but grounds standing in rational agency, which creates problems for infants, the cognitively disabled and animals. <a href="#s4">§4</a> handles these through tiers.</p>
        <p><strong>Virtue ethics.</strong> It supports the humility the framework requires, but it is weak on structural questions.</p>
        <p><strong>Justice as fairness and capabilities.</strong> The capabilities approach (Sen 2009; Nussbaum 2006) supplies substantive accounts of what is at stake for people. The framework is procedural and can draw on these to calibrate severity (<a href="#s9">§9</a>).</p>
        <p><strong>Rights.</strong> Rights are outputs, and the framework treats them as external constraints. What it adds is an account of when and how actors must justify influence in the space that rights and regulation leave open.</p>
        <p><strong>Realism.</strong> The realist who denies that power owes anything to the weak cannot be refuted from within the framework, because the framework is internal to the justificatory tradition (<a href="#s2-7">§2.7</a>). The framework does not attempt such a refutation, and it does not argue that the realist who argues for realism already presupposes J, because that would be a transcendental argument, and the framework's argument for J is abductive, not transcendental (<a href="#s2">§2.3</a>, <a href="#s15">§15.1</a>).</p>

        <h4 id="s2-4b">2.4b The power objection</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">A procedural framework that tells the powerful how to justify themselves may simply give them a new vocabulary for doing what they were going to do anyway.</p>

        <p><strong>The objection.</strong> The framework asks powerful actors to explain, to submit to review, to justify. But the powerful are precisely the actors with the resources to perform these rituals convincingly. Proceduralism, on this objection, does not constrain power. It <em>legitimates</em> power by giving it a veneer of accountability.</p>

        <p><strong>The reply, in three parts.</strong></p>

        <p><em>First, the objection is partly correct.</em> Proceduralism <em>can</em> legitimate power. That is why <a href="#s11">§11.4</a> treats T5 as a precondition rather than a diagnosis. <a href="#s14-7">§14.7</a> shows the mechanism at work.</p>

        <p><em>Second, the objection is not specific to proceduralism.</em> Any normative framework can be captured by the powerful. The question is whether a framework has internal resources against capture. The framework's claim is that T5-as-precondition is such a resource: it does not prevent capture, but it makes the capture visible, and it downgrades the legitimacy of a captured process.</p>

        <p><em>Third, the framework's modesty is not an admission of failure.</em> A framework that claimed to <em>solve</em> the problem of power would be either naive or dangerous. The framework claims to <em>handle</em> it.</p>

        <p><strong>What this does not do.</strong> It does not answer the objection fully. A sufficiently powerful actor can capture the publication requirement itself. What the framework can do is make the capture visible. The objection is the second-hardest the framework faces, after the realist objection in <a href="#objections">objection 1</a>.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-5">2.5 Traditions that start elsewhere</h4>
        <p>Ubuntu-inspired moral theory (Metz 2007), Confucian role ethics (Ames 2011) and many Indigenous relational ontologies (Watts 2013; de la Cadena 2015; Kimmerer 2013) begin from relational personhood and obligations to kin, community and land. They tend to arrive at related demands on those who hold power over dependents, by different grounds. The framework treats them as additional considerations pointing toward its operative core — not as independent proofs.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-6">2.6 Costs</h4>
        <p>J is thin, so it underdetermines outcomes. The framework presupposes a minimal commitment to the equal standing of those affected; it is therefore not neutral among all comprehensive doctrines. Its aim is breadth of acceptability rather than neutrality. And because the ground is an abductive argument rather than a proof, it is open to the objection that the abduction is weak. The reply is the modular design of <a href="#s15">§15.5</a>.</p>

        <h4 id="s2-7">2.7 The framework's normative location, stated openly</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">A framework that claims breadth while relying on a specific tradition is not honest about what it is.</p>

        <p>The framework is <strong>internal to a justificatory tradition</strong>. It takes for granted that those subject to arrangements have standing to demand reasons for them. It does not attempt to argue someone into that position from outside. A reader who rejects the justificatory tradition altogether will not be persuaded by anything in this essay.</p>

        <p><strong>Which tradition, exactly.</strong> The justificatory tradition includes a <em>thick</em> version (Habermas, Apel, the fuller Forst) and a <em>thin</em> version. The thick version requires actual rational consensus, mutual acceptability under idealised conditions, and a specific theory of communicative rationality. The thin version requires only that affected parties have some moral weight that cannot be reduced to their instrumental value to the powerful. The framework needs only the thin version.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework actually requires.</strong> Three claims, of decreasing thickness:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Thin standing.</strong> Affected parties have some moral weight that cannot be reduced to their instrumental value to the powerful. <em>Widely shared.</em></li>
          <li><strong>Semi-thick procedural claim.</strong> Arrangements that impose significant costs on affected parties must be justified to them, not merely to a third party. <em>Justificatory-tradition-specific, but not uniquely so.</em></li>
          <li><strong>Framework-specific claim.</strong> Justification can take the form of meaningful exit, independent review, or contestation — disjunctively. <em>Specific to this framework.</em></li>
        </ol>

        <p><strong>Why thin standing is a reasonable starting point.</strong> The essay does not attempt to prove thin standing from nothing. It offers three considerations in its support, which are not proofs but are more than assertions:</p>

        <p>First, thin standing is the minimal normative position compatible with taking the affected party seriously as a source of reasons rather than as an object to be managed. To deny it is to say that the affected party's account of its own condition carries no weight — that whatever it says, the actor may substitute its own view. This is a coherent position (the realist holds it), but it is a position that must be argued for, not assumed. The essay does not assume it.</p>

        <p>Second, thin standing is widely shared across traditions that otherwise disagree. The Kantian, the consequentialist, the capability theorist, the care ethicist, the republican, and the relational ontologist all accept that affected parties have some moral weight that cannot be reduced to instrumental value. The framework does not require agreement on why — only on that.</p>

        <p>Third, thin standing is a claim that the affected party can press. It is not a gift the powerful give the weak; it is a claim the weak can make. The framework's whole procedural machinery — answerability, contestation, review — is designed to make that claim usable. If thin standing were merely a moral attitude, the machinery would be unnecessary. It is necessary because the claim is real.</p>

        <p><strong>Why J follows from thin standing, given B.</strong> Given thin standing and B, J is a reasonable specification of what the claim amounts to in practice. A party with standing to demand reasons, whose reasons can be overridden only by evidence the actor does not control, has a claim that the actor's influence be open to exit, review, or contestation. The disjunctive form of J (§2.1) is the weakest form that satisfies this claim.</p>

        <p><strong>What this means for the abductive argument (§2.3).</strong> The abductive argument is not an argument <em>for</em> the thin standing claim or <em>for</em> the semi-thick procedural claim. Those are stated as premises. The abductive argument is an argument for J <em>given</em> those premises.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>The framework is internal to a justificatory tradition, but only to its <strong>thin</strong> version.</li>
            <li>Three claims, of decreasing thickness: thin standing, semi-thick procedural claim, framework-specific disjunction.</li>
            <li>Three considerations support thin standing as a starting point without proving it from nothing.</li>
            <li>The abductive argument is for J <em>given</em> the premises, not for the premises themselves.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h4 id="s2-8">2.8 Adversarial tests: what Pettit, Forst and Jonas would object</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">Section 1.5 and Table 1 show what the framework adds to the four traditions it engages. §2.7 shows its normative location within one of them. Neither shows what those traditions would say <em>against</em> it.</p>

        <h5>Pettit: the epistemic framing undercuts the republican point</h5>

        <p><strong>The objection.</strong> The framework's <em>epistemic</em> framing of J (§2.1) undercuts the republican point. Pettit is not primarily an epistemologist: his claim is that domination is a <em>political</em> fact, not an <em>epistemic</em> one.</p>

        <p><strong>The reply, in two parts.</strong> First, the epistemic framing is an <em>entry point</em>, not a <em>foundation</em>. Because domination is invisible to the actor without contestation, contestation is a <em>necessary</em> safeguard. Second, the framework does not <em>replace</em> the republican claim with the epistemic one. It <em>supplements</em> it.</p>

        <h5>Forst: the thin/thick distinction is unstable</h5>

        <p><strong>The objection.</strong> Forst's right to justification is already thin: it is the right to <em>demand and receive</em> reasons. If Forst already has the thin version, what does the framework add?</p>

        <p><strong>The reply.</strong> The framework adds <em>allocation</em>. Forst says that the affected party has a right to justification; the framework says <em>who</em> must give it, <em>in what form</em>, <em>by when</em>, and <em>with what safeguards</em>.</p>

        <h5>Jonas: the framework is too near-sighted</h5>

        <p><strong>The objection.</strong> Jonas is concerned with the species and with the future; the framework is concerned with present affected parties.</p>

        <p><strong>The reply.</strong> The framework is a <em>special case</em> of Jonas's ethics, applied to <em>contemporary structural influence</em>, and it differs from Jonas in one specific respect: it does not require Jonas's metaphysical grounding. What the framework <em>inherits</em> from Jonas is the <em>capacity-based structure</em> of responsibility and the <em>forward-looking</em> orientation, which §12.5 applies to future generations.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Positioning ("what we add") is not engagement ("what they would object").</li>
            <li>Pettit would say the epistemic framing undercuts the republican point. The framework replies: the epistemic framing is an entry point, not a foundation.</li>
            <li>Forst would say the thin/thick distinction is unstable. The framework replies: the addition is <em>allocation</em>, not thickness.</li>
            <li>Jonas would say the framework is near-sighted. The framework replies: it is a special case of Jonas's ethics without Jonas's metaphysics.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s3">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">3.</span>The Exercised-Capacity Principle</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Influence triggers the duty, stakes and contestation scale it, and capacity allocates it.</p>

        <div class="key-prop" data-label="The Exercised-Capacity Principle">
          <p>An actor that exercises or maintains discretionary influence over the conditions of others' lives owes them an account of it when exit and review are unavailable or ineffective. How much it owes scales with the stakes and with the affected parties' difficulty in contesting the influence (<a href="#s7">§7</a>). The burden of establishing the mechanisms through which the account is given falls on actors in proportion to their capacity to establish them (<a href="#s8">§8.2</a>).</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Trigger:</strong> exercised or maintained influence, <em>and</em> the unavailability or ineffectiveness of exit and review. <strong>Scale:</strong> stakes and contestation deficit. <strong>Allocation:</strong> capacity to discharge.</p>

        <h4 id="s3-1">3.1 Why not the rivals</h4>
        <p><strong>Causal responsibility</strong> is necessary for attribution but insufficient for structural influence (Thompson 1980). <strong>Fault-based responsibility</strong> concerns blame; answerability does not presuppose fault (Shoemaker 2011). <strong>Relational responsibility</strong> is real, and the framework retains it, but relational duties alone say nothing about anonymous influence. <strong>Consent</strong> is valuable where meaningful, but under asymmetry it can be manufactured or coerced. <strong>The social connection model</strong> (Young 2011) is the closest rival, and the framework is largely compatible with it; it adds a discretionary trigger and a specification of levels.</p>

        <h4 id="s3-2">3.2 The cost: answerability handles power; it does not solve it</h4>
        <p>A principle that makes the powerful responsible risks being used by them as a badge of legitimacy. The framework does not claim to eliminate this risk. Three constraints apply: responsibility is not authority (<a href="#s8">§8</a>, <a href="#s16">§16</a>), review must meet independence conditions (<a href="#s11">§11</a>), and the risk is tested directly in <a href="#s15">§15</a>.</p>

        <p><strong>Handling, not solving.</strong> The framework's aim is not to solve the problem of power — no framework can. Its aim is to convert the problem from an unstructured condition into a set of design constraints that can be observed, contested and revised.</p>

        <h4 id="s3-3">3.3 The exercised-capacity principle alongside the social connection model: an illustrative case</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">A general claim about allocation is worth little without a case in which it does work that the closest rival cannot.</p>

        <p><strong>The case.</strong> A recommendation algorithm on a large platform amplifies content that increases political polarisation. Who bears the duty to answer for it?</p>

        <p><strong>Young's answer.</strong> Everyone who participates in the structure that produces the harm shares forward-looking responsibility.</p>

        <p><strong>ECP's answer.</strong> The trigger is exercised discretionary influence, <em>combined with</em> the unavailability or ineffectiveness of exit and review. The users have no discretion over the ranking code, and no meaningful exit from the platform's network effects. The advertisers have discretion over whether to fund, not over how the ranking works. The engineers and executives have discretion over both, and the users cannot exit or review the ranking they produce. So the duty attaches, at Level 3, to the design team and the executives, and at Level 2 to the regulators who could have required audit but did not.</p>

        <p><strong>What ECP may add in this case.</strong> Young's model has been criticised for exactly this: if everyone is responsible, no one is. ECP keeps the structural insight and adds the allocation that the model lacks.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Young's social connection model identifies shared responsibility; ECP proposes a more granular allocation.</li>
            <li>The advantage is demonstrated in a concrete case, not asserted in general terms.</li>
            <li>ECP does not claim to have refuted Young; it claims to have entered a contest it can lose.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h4 id="s3-4">3.4 Distributed influence and delegated discretion</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">A principle that only handles face-to-face influence is not a principle for the conditions most people actually live under. ECP must show how it allocates responsibility when influence is spread across a chain, a system, or an organisation.</p>

        <p><strong>Five modes of contribution.</strong> In distributed cases, the question is not only who exercised influence, but in what way each party contributed:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Causing.</strong> The party's action produces the effect directly.</li>
          <li><strong>Enabling.</strong> The party creates the conditions under which another party can produce the effect (funding, infrastructure, legal permission, data).</li>
          <li><strong>Controlling.</strong> The party retains the ability to stop, redirect, or authorise the effect, even if it does not itself act.</li>
          <li><strong>Foreseeing.</strong> The party knows or could reasonably know that the effect will occur.</li>
          <li><strong>Benefiting.</strong> The party gains from the effect without contributing to it.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>How ECP allocates.</strong> Causing and controlling are sufficient for the trigger. Enabling is sufficient only where the enabler retains the ability to withdraw the enabling condition at tolerable cost. Foreseeing is not sufficient on its own, but it raises the level. Benefiting is not sufficient on its own, but it generates a duty of inquiry.</p>

        <p><strong>The delegation question.</strong> A party cannot escape ECP by delegating the decision if it retains control over the conditions under which the decision is made. Three conditions determine whether delegation succeeds or fails:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>The delegator retains the ability to revoke.</strong> If the delegator can withdraw the authority, change the terms, or remove the delegate at tolerable cost, the delegator retains exercised influence.</li>
          <li><strong>The delegator sets the frame.</strong> If the delegator defines the objectives, the constraints, the success criteria, or the data on which the delegate acts, the delegator's influence is not diminished by the delegation.</li>
          <li><strong>The delegation is not a genuine transfer of discretion.</strong> If the delegate has no real alternatives, the discretion remains with the delegator, whatever the formal structure says.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><strong>Five contributory roles, and how they map onto responsibility.</strong></p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Causing</strong> generates primary responsibility. The party whose action produces the effect directly is the primary duty-bearer. But "directly" is often contested.</li>
          <li><strong>Enabling</strong> generates secondary responsibility, contingent on the capacity to withdraw.</li>
          <li><strong>Controlling</strong> generates primary responsibility even where the controller does not act. A board that retains the power to reverse a decision is answerable for the decision, whether or not it exercises that power.</li>
          <li><strong>Foreseeing</strong> generates a duty of inquiry. A party that knows or could reasonably know about an effect has a duty to investigate, but not necessarily to prevent.</li>
          <li><strong>Benefiting</strong> generates a duty of inquiry once the party knows.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>The problem of diffusion.</strong> In complex systems, responsibility can diffuse until no party is answerable. The framework's response is not to insist that some party must be answerable — that would be a normative claim the framework cannot support in every case. It is to insist that the diffusion be located and named. Where no party has the capacity to discharge the duty, the framework says so (§14.5e).</p>

        <p><strong>When responsibility changes over time.</strong> A party's responsibility is not fixed at the moment of action. It changes as the party's capacity and knowledge change. A regulator that lacked the capacity to audit a system in year one, but has the capacity in year five, becomes answerable for the failure to audit in year five. A platform that did not know about a harm in year one, but knows in year five, becomes answerable for what it does with that knowledge.</p>

        <p><strong>A worked case: the supply chain.</strong> A garment brand sources from a factory that sources from a subcontractor that employs home workers. The home workers' conditions are shaped by the brand's pricing, delivery deadlines, and audit regime. Who bears the ECP duty?</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The home workers</strong> exercise no discretionary influence over their conditions. No duty.</li>
          <li><strong>The subcontractor</strong> exercises discretion over hours and pay, but under constraints set by the factory. Level 1 duty; Level 2 if the factory's constraints are such that the subcontractor cannot refuse them at tolerable cost.</li>
          <li><strong>The factory</strong> exercises discretion over which subcontractor it uses and what terms it sets. Level 2 duty; Level 3 if the terms are so constrained by the brand that the factory cannot refuse.</li>
          <li><strong>The brand</strong> sets the frame: prices, deadlines, audit criteria. It retains the ability to revoke, to set the frame, and to restore discretion to the factory. By the three conditions above, delegation to the factory is formal only. Level 3 duty.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What this does not do.</strong> It does not solve the collective action problem. Where no party can revoke, set the frame, or restore discretion, ECP does not generate a determinate allocation. In such cases the duty is distributed in a way that no single party can discharge alone, and the appropriate response is collective (§4.6, §10), not individual.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Distributed influence is handled through the five modes of contribution (§3.4).</li>
            <li>Delegation is genuine only if the delegator retains none of three capacities: revoke, set the frame, restore discretion. If any of the three remains, ECP allocates to the delegator.</li>
            <li>Where no party can discharge the duty — the genuinely polycentric case — ECP does not generate a determinate allocation.</li>
            <li>Responsibility changes over time as capacity and knowledge change.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <div class="part-divider" id="part-ii">
        <p class="part-divider__label">Part II</p>
        <h2 class="part-divider__title">Who Is Owed an Account</h2>
      </div>

      <section id="s4">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">4.</span>Standing Under Uncertainty</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Incomprehensibility is not evidence of unimportance, but neither is it evidence of importance.</p>

        <h4 id="s4-1">4.1 Four tiers</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tier A: agents.</strong> Beings with normative agency (persons).</li>
          <li><strong>Tier B: welfare subjects.</strong> Beings with interests of their own: sentient animals, and humans who lack agency.</li>
          <li><strong>Tier C: uncertain candidates.</strong> Entities for which there is a non-negligible, evidence-based probability of Tier B status (Birch 2024).</li>
          <li><strong>Tier D: non-subjects.</strong> Entities with no credible interests of their own: stones, rivers and ecosystems as such. The framework claims no direct standing for them.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="s4-2">4.2 The plausibility threshold</h4>
        <p>The test for Tier C is whether there is a credible, evidence-based reason to think the entity has states that matter to it. Without such a threshold, precaution generates reasons everywhere and guides nowhere (Sunstein 2005).</p>

        <h4 id="s4-2b">4.2b Five properties, not one</h4>
        <p>The framework's Tier A–D structure is often read as if it were a ranking by "how much" a being matters. It is not. The tiers distinguish five properties that are often conflated:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Moral status.</strong> Whether the being has interests that count in the framework's assessment at all. Tier A–C have it; Tier D does not.</li>
          <li><strong>Interests.</strong> What the being has reason to want or avoid, given its form of life. A sentient animal has interests (avoiding pain); a plant has interests in a broader sense (access to light, water) but not necessarily in the sense that generates a claim on the framework.</li>
          <li><strong>Sentience.</strong> Whether the being has subjective experience. This is a sub-question of interest. Tier B requires at least probable sentience; Tier C is the case where sentience is possible but uncertain.</li>
          <li><strong>Vulnerability.</strong> Whether the being can be harmed by the exercise of discretionary influence.</li>
          <li><strong>Capacity to express.</strong> Whether the being can express preferences, objections, or refusals. This is not the same as moral status: infants have moral status without capacity to express; some AI systems may have capacity to express without moral status.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>These five properties are related but not identical. A framework that equates them will either over-attribute (treating every complex system as having moral status) or under-attribute (treating every being that cannot speak as having no claims). The tier structure is an attempt to hold them apart.</p>

        <p><strong>Precaution under uncertainty.</strong> Tier C is where the framework's precaution is most active. The plausibility threshold (§4.2) is not "any possibility"; it is "a credible, evidence-based reason to think the entity has states that matter to it." This threshold does real work: it excludes the stone (§4.3) while including entities whose sentience is uncertain but plausible. What the threshold does not do is specify exactly what precaution is required once it is met. The framework's answer: the same safeguards as for Tier B, adjusted for uncertainty. This means representation (§4.6) is required where preferences cannot be determined; the burden of proof for actions that may harm the entity is on the actor; reversibility is preferred where possible; and records are preserved so that later evidence can correct the assessment.</p>

        <p><strong>The projection problem.</strong> Representation of uncertain entities carries a specific risk: that the representative projects their own values, preferences, or categories onto the represented party. The §4.6 conditions are designed to reduce this, but they do not eliminate it. A representative can satisfy all six conditions and still project. The framework's response is not to pretend otherwise. It is to make the projection visible: where the entity's preferences cannot be determined, the representative's account is a claim, not a fact, and it must be reviewable as a claim.</p>

        <h4 id="s4-3">4.3 The stone, restated</h4>
        <p>The stone marks the edge of the framework's claims. For beings that meet the threshold, our failure to understand their way of being is no reason to dismiss their interests. For the stone, which does not meet it, the framework claims no standing and says so.</p>

        <h4 id="s4-4">4.4 Artificial systems</h4>
        <p>Answerability for the influence of AI systems falls on the humans and organisations that design, deploy and maintain them. Whether some artificial systems may themselves be Tier C candidates is contested, and the framework does not answer it.</p>

        <h4 id="s4-5">4.5 Perspectival plurality</h4>
        <p>For beings in Tiers A–C, their way of experiencing the world may be difficult or impossible for us to reach. That a perspective is inaccessible is not evidence that it is absent (Fricker 2007).</p>

        <h4 id="s4-6">4.6 Representation</h4>
        <p>Some affected parties cannot take part directly: future generations, infants, animals, communities that lack institutional voice. Representation is legitimate only if (i) representatives are chosen through a process that can itself be contested, (ii) they state the basis on which they claim to know the interests concerned, (iii) where interests are contested there is more than one representative or perspective, (iv) they can be removed, (v) they are neither the actor nor dependent on the actor for appointment or funding, and (vi) they represent uncertainty rather than substituting invented certainty.</p>

        <p><strong>(vii) Collective contestation must not undermine individual contestation.</strong> Where a collective procedure is used to represent affected parties, the procedure must preserve the ability of individuals <em>within</em> the collective to contest the collective's decisions, and to seek independent review of them where the collective's own procedures fail.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Four tiers of standing: agents, welfare subjects, uncertain candidates, non-subjects.</li>
            <li>Tier C requires a <strong>plausibility threshold</strong>.</li>
            <li>Five properties are distinguished, not conflated: moral status, interests, sentience, vulnerability, capacity to express.</li>
            <li>Representation of silent parties is legitimate only under six conditions.</li>
            <li>Collective contestation must not undermine individual contestation.</li>
            <li>The projection problem in representation is a real risk, not eliminable; the framework makes it visible rather than pretending otherwise.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s5">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">5.</span>Interdependence, Difference and Non-Domination</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Existence is relational. What follows from that is not an ethics, but the ground on which an ethics must stand.</p>

        <p>Living beings, institutions, technologies and ecosystems exist through relations. Some of these relations are dependence, some are influence, some are interaction, some are competition, some are conflict, some are destruction, and some are mutual creation. This is the descriptive claim the essay calls <strong>universal symbiosis</strong>: not a claim that the universe is harmonious, moral, or guided by purpose, but a claim that nothing exists — no being, no institution, no technology, no ecosystem — except through relations with other things. The relations are not optional additions to isolated existents. They are what the existents are made of.</p>

        <p>Universal symbiosis is a worldview, not an ethics. It says nothing about how relations <em>should</em> be arranged. It is compatible with mutualism and with parasitism, with care and with extraction, with cooperation and with domination. This is important, because the essay's central normative claim — that certain ways of arranging relations are unacceptable — is often mistaken for a claim that follows directly from the relational nature of existence. It does not. The move from universal symbiosis to normative symbiosis is the essay's most important argumentative step, and §2.0a and §5.3 make it explicitly. Universal symbiosis describes what exists. Normative symbiosis specifies what is owed. The two are not the same, and the essay keeps them apart from here on.</p>

        <h4 id="s5-0a">5.0a Three relations, not one</h4>
        <p>Universal symbiosis is a single word for at least three distinct relations, and they should not be conflated:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Causal influence.</strong> A affects B. The relation is one-directional or mutual, but it is a relation of effect. A factory's emissions affect a downwind community's air. The community does not cause the factory's emissions, but it is affected by them.</li>
          <li><strong>Dependence.</strong> A requires B for its continued existence, development, or functioning. A child depends on its caregivers. An ecosystem depends on its water cycle. The relation is asymmetric in a specific way: A's flourishing requires B, but B may not require A.</li>
          <li><strong>Ecological belonging.</strong> A is part of a system that has properties A does not have alone. A species belongs to an ecosystem. An individual belongs to a community. The relation is constitutive: A is partly what it is because of its place in the system.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>These three relations can come apart. An actor can affect a party without depending on it (the factory affects the community but does not depend on it). An actor can depend on a party without affecting it in the relevant sense (the patient depends on the nurse but does not affect the nurse's health). An actor can belong to a system without exercising discretion over it (the species belongs to the ecosystem without controlling it).</p>

        <p>The ethical question the essay addresses is not 'does A exist in relation to B?' — the answer is always yes. The question is 'when does A's relation to B create a duty of answerability?' That question is addressed in §3, §6 and §7, and its answer is not 'always' — it is 'when A exercises discretionary influence over B's conditions and B cannot exit or contest it.'</p>

        <p><strong>The local and the systemic.</strong> Universal symbiosis has a structural consequence that the essay's procedural machinery is designed to handle: local actions can produce systemic effects, and systemic changes can produce local effects. A change in a platform's ranking code is a local action in one sense (a few engineers change a few lines of code) and a systemic effect in another (millions of users' information environments shift). A change in a species' population is a systemic effect in one sense (the ecosystem's composition changes) and a local effect in another (individual animals' conditions change). The essay's activation rule (§7) and its account of distributed influence (§3.4) are attempts to locate responsibility in cases where the local and the systemic cannot be separated cleanly. They do not solve the problem. They locate it.</p>

        <p>The word "symbiosis" is used in this essay in a single, normative sense from here on: it names a <strong>floor</strong> for relations of dependence — the minimum conditions under which dependence is not exploited, difference is not forced into sameness, and the dependent party keeps the conditions for continued existence, development and participation (§10). This is <strong>normative symbiosis</strong>, and it is distinct from universal symbiosis in the way just stated. Parasitism, extraction, and domination are as much a part of universal symbiosis as mutualism is; the framework's claim is that they are unacceptable, not that they do not occur.</p>

        <p>Answerability (<a href="#s3">§3</a>) is the procedure by which this floor is pursued. §5.2 argues that under conditions of asymmetric influence, answerability is not just the road to symbiosis, but the <em>heightened epistemic safeguard</em> for knowing whether symbiosis has been achieved.</p>

        <p>Harmony enforced by silencing, homogenising or sacrificing the vulnerable is not normative symbiosis. Difference is not a defect to be corrected. The asymmetry also runs both ways: the more capable may misunderstand the less capable, and the less capable cannot audit the more capable.</p>

        <h4 id="s5-1">5.1 Making normative symbiosis measurable (or admitting it is not)</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">If the framework states defeaters for its ground and failure conditions for its activation rule, its own ideal cannot be exempt from assessment.</p>

        <p>Normative symbiosis is the floor for relations of dependence — not the descriptive fact that relations exist, but the minimum conditions under which a relation of dependence is acceptable.</p>

        <p><strong>Three indicators, specified conceptually apart from answerability.</strong></p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Non-exploitation (SY1).</strong> The dependent party's continued participation does not require it to accept terms that it would reject if it had exit at tolerable cost. Measured by: the gap between stated preferences and accepted terms, adjusted for exit costs. Note that the indicator is defined by the <em>dependent party's</em> stated preferences, not by the more powerful party's assessment of what those preferences should be. Where the dependent party cannot state preferences — infants, animals, non-communicating persons, future generations — SY1 is not assessed by the absence of stated preferences. It is assessed through the representational substitutes in §4.6 and the structural indicators in §15.2b.</li>
          <li><strong>Non-assimilation (SY2).</strong> The dependent party's difference is not treated as a defect to be corrected. Measured by: whether the actor's interventions aim at <em>removing the difference</em> or at <em>removing the burden the difference carries</em>. A programme that seeks to make deaf children hearing is assimilation; a programme that provides sign language and accessible environments is not.</li>
          <li><strong>Continued possibility (SY3).</strong> The dependent party retains the conditions for continued existence, development and participation. Measured by: whether F1–F5 (<a href="#s10">§10</a>) hold over time, not just at a single decision.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Cases where one indicator fails while others hold.</strong></p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>SY1 fails, SY2 and SY3 hold.</strong> A workfare programme requires participants to accept low-wage work as a condition of benefits. SY1 fails: participants accept terms they would reject at tolerable exit cost. SY2 holds: the programme does not treat participants' difference as a defect — it provides work, not "re-education." SY3 holds: participants retain the conditions for continued existence and development. The relation is exploitative (SY1 fails) even though it is not assimilating and not foreclosing.</li>
          <li><strong>SY2 fails, SY1 and SY3 hold.</strong> A language programme for deaf children teaches them to speak and discourages sign language. SY1 holds: parents accept voluntarily. SY3 holds: children develop language capacity. SY2 fails: the programme treats deafness as a defect to be corrected. The relation is assimilating even though it is not exploitative and not foreclosing.</li>
          <li><strong>SY3 fails, SY1 and SY2 hold.</strong> A training programme for adults with cognitive disabilities places them in jobs that pay adequately and does not treat their disability as a defect. But the jobs are dead-end, with no possibility of advancement. The relation forecloses development even though it is neither exploitative nor assimilating.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>These cases show that the indicators do specific work: each catches a failure that the others miss. They also show that no single indicator is sufficient.</p>

        <p><strong>Why this matters for the framework's integrity.</strong> The framework requires actors to answer for their influence. It would be incoherent for the framework's own ideal to be exempt from that requirement.</p>

        <p><strong>Are SY1–SY3 independent of each other?</strong> Not fully. But they are independent enough that no single one can substitute for the other two:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>They come apart in actual cases</strong> (as the three examples above show).</li>
          <li><strong>They have different measurement procedures.</strong> SY1 requires elicitation of stated preferences. SY2 requires classification of intervention design. SY3 requires longitudinal observation of F1–F5.</li>
          <li><strong>They have different defeaters.</strong> SY1 is defeated if stated preferences are found to be adaptive. SY2 is defeated if design classification is shown to be a function of the actor's self-description. SY3 is defeated if F1–F5 are shown to hold trivially.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>SY1's limits as an indicator.</strong> SY1 is a necessary but not a sufficient indicator of non-exploitation. It compares two facts — stated preferences and accepted terms — but if the preferences are adaptive, the comparison can converge for the wrong reason. The framework's response is to weight structural indicators (dissent channels, counterfactual improvement) more heavily than SY1 where they diverge (§15.2b). How the framework weighs stated preferences against these indicators, and how it avoids the paternalism risk, is set out in §5.2.</p>

        <p><strong>What "exit at tolerable cost" means when alternatives are absent.</strong> Exit cost is the total cost to the affected party of leaving the relation: material costs (loss of income, housing, healthcare), social costs (loss of community, status, relationships), psychological costs (loss of identity, security), and opportunity costs (loss of future options). "Tolerable" means the cost does not violate the party's F1–F5 protections (§10). If exit would leave the party without the conditions for continued existence, development, or participation, exit is not at tolerable cost — it is not a real option at all. Where no real alternative exists, SY1 cannot be assessed by the party's stated preference for exit, because there is nothing to prefer to. The framework's response is to assess SY1 through the structural indicators (iii and iv in §15.2b) and through counterfactual improvement: if exit were made available at tolerable cost, would the party's condition improve? This is not a perfect substitute for the party's own preference — it is the best available measure where the party's preference cannot be formed.</p>

        <p><strong>What SY1–SY3 do not show.</strong> A relation that satisfies all three is not therefore good. A relation that fails one is not therefore beyond repair — it is a relation where a specific failure has been identified, and where the failure can be addressed if the parties choose to. The indicators are diagnostic, not verdictive.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Universal symbiosis (descriptive) is distinguished from normative symbiosis (prescriptive) throughout (§2.0a, §5).</li>
            <li>Normative symbiosis is a floor, specified through three observable indicators: non-exploitation (SY1), non-assimilation (SY2), continued possibility (SY3).</li>
            <li>SY1–SY3 are not fully independent, but they are independent enough: they come apart in actual cases (three worked examples above), have different measurement procedures, and have different defeaters.</li>
            <li>SY1's circularity problem is handled by the T5-precondition and structural indicators; SY1 is weighted below (iii)/(iv) in §15.2b.</li>
            <li>"Exit at tolerable cost" means the cost does not violate F1–F5. Where no real alternative exists, SY1 is assessed through structural indicators and counterfactual improvement.</li>
            <li>Indicators are diagnostic, not verdictive.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h4 id="s5-2">5.2 The epistemic burden of asymmetric influence</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">Power does not make knowledge impossible. It changes what counts as adequate evidence.</p>

        <p>Where one party controls the terms of dependence, it may also control the categories, information and measures through which the relation is evaluated. The more powerful party can therefore mistake the absence of visible objection for consent, compliance for flourishing, or institutional approval for legitimacy.</p>

        <p>This is not a claim that powerful actors are incapable of understanding others, or that every judgment they make is distorted. Nor does it imply that the less powerful party is always correct. The claim is narrower: <strong>asymmetric influence creates a heightened risk of epistemic self-confirmation</strong>.</p>

        <aside class="key-prop" data-label="The epistemic burden principle">
          <p>Where an actor exercises substantial discretionary influence over parties who lack effective means of exit or contestation, the actor bears a heightened burden to support claims about the effects and legitimacy of that influence with evidence that is not controlled solely by the actor, and with meaningful opportunities for affected parties or their legitimate representatives to correct the account.</p>
          <p>This is a rebuttable burden of inquiry and justification. It is not a claim that answerability is the only route to knowledge, that affected parties are infallible, or that procedure is sufficient for justice.</p>
        </aside>

        <h5>Argument</h5>
        <p><strong>1. The object of assessment is partly first-personal.</strong> Whether a relation is exploitative, assimilating or agency-preserving cannot always be determined from aggregate outcomes alone. The experiences and judgments of affected parties are therefore relevant evidence, though not the only evidence.</p>

        <p><strong>2. Asymmetry affects the production and interpretation of evidence.</strong> The more powerful party may set the available options, define success, control records, determine which complaints are legible, and select the time horizon over which outcomes are measured. These mechanisms can produce systematic blind spots without requiring malice.</p>

        <p><strong>3. Self-assessment is therefore insufficient as a default.</strong> When the actor controls both the influence and the evidential frame used to evaluate it, its conclusion is not independent confirmation.</p>

        <p><strong>4. The appropriate response is plural correction, not a single privileged voice.</strong> Affected parties should be able to correct descriptions of their own interests and experiences. Independent reviewers can test factual claims and identify conflicts within groups. Representatives may be necessary where direct participation is impossible, but their authority must itself be contestable.</p>

        <p><strong>5. This yields a practical principle of answerability.</strong> The greater the discretionary influence, severity, entrenchment and deficit of contestation, the less acceptable it is for the actor to rely on its own assessment alone.</p>

        <h5>What follows — and what does not</h5>
        <p>The argument supports a <em>presumption against epistemic closure</em>: where power is asymmetric and affected parties cannot effectively contest, the powerful party must not treat its own view as conclusive. This is not a new principle. It is B (§2.0) applied to the specific question of what the actor owes when it controls both the influence and the evidential frame. The epistemic burden principle is B made operational.</p>

        <h5>How adaptive preferences are weighed — and the paternalism risk</h5>

        <p><strong>The problem.</strong> A party may report satisfaction because it has adapted its preferences to a constrained situation. SY1 takes stated preferences seriously; but taken alone, it cannot distinguish genuine acceptance from adapted acceptance. §15.2b treats SY1 as the weakest of the four confirmation sources for exactly this reason.</p>

        <p><strong>How the framework weighs.</strong> When SY1 and the structural indicators diverge, the framework does not resolve the divergence by preferring one over the other wholesale. It weighs them across four dimensions:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>Consistency across time.</strong> Do the party's stated preferences match what it has said or done over time? A sudden convergence is weaker evidence than a stable one.</li>
          <li><strong>Consistency across contexts.</strong> Does the party express the same preferences in low-stakes contexts where the relation is not at issue?</li>
          <li><strong>Availability of the vocabulary for objection.</strong> If dissent channels exist and have been used, stated acceptance is more probative than if no such vocabulary exists.</li>
          <li><strong>Counterfactual improvement.</strong> If exit is made available at tolerable cost and the party's reported satisfaction does not change, the satisfaction is more probative.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>None of these four is decisive on its own. Together they form the framework's answer to the question <em>"how much weight should be given to stated preferences?"</em>: as much as the evidence about their formation supports, and no more.</p>

        <p><strong>The paternalism risk, named explicitly.</strong> The framework's position on adaptive preferences carries an obvious risk: that a more powerful party will use the possibility of adaptive preferences to override what the affected party actually says, on the grounds that the party <em>"doesn't really know what it wants."</em> The framework does not license this. Three constraints prevent it:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The party's stated preferences remain the default.</strong> Adaptive preferences are a reason to look for further evidence, not a reason to substitute the actor's own view for the party's.</li>
          <li><strong>The framework does not authorize an outsider to speak for the party.</strong> Where the party's preferences appear adapted, the framework's response is to look for structural evidence, not to allow a representative to declare what the party <em>"really"</em> wants.</li>
          <li><strong>The framework treats the party's own account as evidence, not as noise.</strong> Where there is no further evidence, the framework defaults to the party's stated preferences, not to the more powerful party's assessment.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What this does not solve.</strong> The framework cannot, in general, distinguish a party that has genuinely accepted its conditions from a party whose capacity to reject them has been dismantled (§15.2, Limit 1). What it can do is bias the analysis toward the party's stated preferences as the default, add evidence where it is available, and refuse to let the more powerful party substitute its own view for the party's.</p>

        <h5>Relation to existing traditions</h5>
        <p><em>Pettit.</em> Republican freedom as non-domination already makes contestability central. This framework does not claim that Pettit lacks a positive ideal or that it has discovered contestation.</p>
        <p><em>Forst.</em> The right to justification already insists that those subject to norms must be able to demand and assess reasons. This framework asks how the burden of producing evidence, preserving records and funding review should be allocated when the parties' capacities are unequal.</p>
        <p><em>Young.</em> The social connection model already explains responsibility for structural processes without reducing it to individual fault. ECP's proposed addition is a more explicit operational trigger and duty ladder.</p>
        <p><em>Care ethics.</em> Care theorists have developed sophisticated accounts of attentiveness, responsibility, competence and responsiveness. The framework's contribution is to insist that responsiveness must include channels through which the cared-for can challenge the carer's interpretation.</p>

        <h5>Defeaters and tests</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>B1 — No systematic epistemic risk.</strong> If evidence shows that asymmetry does not predictably affect what powerful actors notice, record or credit, the heightened-burden rationale is weakened.</li>
          <li><strong>B2 — Better alternatives.</strong> If another procedure provides equally reliable correction at lower cost, it should replace the proposed safeguards.</li>
          <li><strong>B3 — Participation can worsen knowledge.</strong> If affected-party participation predictably entrenches misinformation, coercion, elite capture or internal silencing, the framework must specify countermeasures.</li>
          <li><strong>B4 — Independent review is not independent.</strong> If reviewers reproduce the same interests, assumptions or exclusions as the actor, formal review does not discharge the burden.</li>
          <li><strong>B5 — Disproportionate burden.</strong> If the costs of the required process exceed its expected protective value in low-stakes cases, the activation thresholds and duty levels must be revised.</li>
        </ul>

