        <h4 id="s5-3">5.3 Why this normative symbiosis, and not another</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">SY1–SY3 are not self-evidently the right indicators of a good relation. This section defends their selection by deriving them from the thin standing claim, and by comparing them to the main rival floors.</p>

        <p><strong>The objection.</strong> §5.1 specifies normative symbiosis through SY1–SY3. But why these three? Why not welfare, or capability, or autonomy, or something else?</p>

        <p><strong>What kind of ideal normative symbiosis is.</strong> It is a <strong>floor</strong>, not a complete theory of the good relation. It specifies the minimum conditions that a relation must meet to count as non-exploitative, non-assimilating, and life-preserving. It does not say that a relation meeting SY1–SY3 is therefore <em>good</em>.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework deliberately leaves open.</strong> A relation can meet every one of SY1–SY3 and F1–F5 and still be cold, indifferent, hollow, or unjust in ways the framework does not measure. The framework does not claim to specify:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Care</strong> in any thick sense — attention, responsiveness, warmth, the orientation of one party toward another's good beyond the minimum of non-extraction.</li>
          <li><strong>Trust</strong> — the confidence that the other party will act well when it is not being watched.</li>
          <li><strong>Meaning</strong> — the sense that the relation contributes to something the parties value beyond their own continuation.</li>
          <li><strong>Community</strong> — the forms of belonging, recognition, and mutual constitution that make a relation more than an arrangement.</li>
          <li><strong>Mutual development</strong> — the parties' growth through the relation, not merely the preservation of their capacity to grow.</li>
          <li><strong>Positive ideals of the good relation</strong> — friendship, love, solidarity, joint projects, shared ends.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Where these ideals are specified</strong>, the framework points to the traditions it engages: the capability approach (Sen 2009; Nussbaum 2006) for a substantive account of what people can do and be; care ethics (Tronto 1993; Held 2006) for an account of attention and responsiveness; the relational traditions (§2.5) for accounts of community and mutual constitution. The framework's contribution is the floor, not the house.</p>

        <h5>Deriving SY1–SY3 from thin standing</h5>

        <p><strong>The premise.</strong> Affected parties have standing to demand reasons for arrangements that shape their conditions (<a href="#s2-7">§2.7</a>, claim 1 and claim 2).</p>

        <p><strong>The question.</strong> Under what conditions is this standing non-vacuous? A standing claim that can be formally acknowledged while being substantively ignored is not a standing claim; it is a courtesy. SY1–SY3 specify the three distinct ways standing can be made vacuous, and therefore the three minimum conditions for it not to be.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>SY1 — Non-exploitation.</strong> If affected parties can demand reasons but are then required to accept terms they would reject if they had exit at tolerable cost, their demand for reasons is hollow. <em>Therefore, for standing to be non-vacuous, SY1 must hold.</em></li>
          <li><strong>SY2 — Non-assimilation.</strong> If affected parties can demand reasons but their difference is treated as a defect to be corrected, their standing is absorbed into assimilation. <em>Therefore, for standing to be non-vacuous, SY2 must hold.</em></li>
          <li><strong>SY3 — Continued possibility.</strong> If affected parties can demand reasons but the conditions of their continued existence, development, or participation do not hold, they have no power to do anything with the reasons they receive. <em>Therefore, for standing to be non-vacuous, SY3 must hold.</em></li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What the derivation shows, and what it does not.</strong> The derivation shows that SY1–SY3 are the three distinct ways standing can fail. It does not show that SY1–SY3 are <em>sufficient</em> for a good relation.</p>

        <p><strong>What kind of floor normative symbiosis is.</strong> It is a <strong>pro tanto floor</strong>: it binds those who accept the framework's premises, and it specifies what those premises <em>require in practice</em>.</p>

        <h5>Compared to rival floors</h5>

        <p><strong>Welfare.</strong> A benevolent despot with perfect welfare provision can still fail SY1 (if the terms are exploitative), SY2 (if difference is treated as defect), and SY3 (if development and participation are foreclosed).</p>

        <p><strong>Capability.</strong> A capability theorist (Sen, Nussbaum) proposes a richer floor. SY1–SY3 are thinner — they specify minimum conditions rather than a substantive list.</p>

        <p><strong>Autonomy.</strong> Autonomy accounts often struggle with cases of legitimate dependence — children, disability, care, illness — where the ideal is not independence but standing-preserving dependence. SY1–SY3 handle these cases directly.</p>

        <p><strong>What would refute the choice of floor.</strong> If a critic can show that a relation clearly failing SY1–SY3 is nonetheless good, or that a relation clearly meeting SY1–SY3 is nonetheless bad in a way the framework cannot detect, the choice of floor should be revised.</p>

        <h5>Six counterexamples, and which principle catches each</h5>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>1. The cold but non-exploitative order.</strong> A relation in which SY1, SY2 and SY3 all hold — but the relation is emotionally empty, indifferent, and offers no warmth or recognition beyond the formal minimum. <em>What catches it:</em> nothing in SY1–SY3. The framework says so explicitly: normative symbiosis is a floor, not a complete ideal.</li>
          <li><strong>2. The order that preserves existence but blocks development.</strong> A relation in which continued existence is secured but the conditions for learning, adaptation, or growth are foreclosed. <em>What catches it:</em> SY3, via F3 (development). The case is a test of whether F3 is read formally or substantively — and the framework should say that it must be read substantively.</li>
          <li><strong>3. The order that preserves future possibilities but controls almost every present choice.</strong> A relation in which the dependent party retains a wide range of future options (F4 holds) but at each moment has almost no choice over what happens to it. <em>What catches it:</em> SY1 partially, if the controlled choices require accepting terms the party would reject at tolerable exit cost. <em>This is the case that the framework most clearly does not fully catch.</em> What it can say is that SY3's temporal dimension requires more than F4 — it requires F1 as an ongoing condition, not just a formal one. But the framework should acknowledge that the case is at the edge of what SY1–SY3 can detect.</li>
          <li><strong>4. The order where preferences are adaptive.</strong> A relation in which the dependent party reports satisfaction, but the satisfaction is itself a product of the relation. <em>What catches it:</em> Limit 1 (§15.2), and §15.2b's structural indicators (iii) and (iv). SY1 alone does not catch it; SY1 weighted with structural indicators does.</li>
          <li><strong>5. The order that protects an ecosystem but harms sentient individuals.</strong> A relation in which the integrity of an ecosystem is preserved at the cost of suffering among sentient beings within it. <em>What catches it:</em> nothing in SY1–SY3 directly. This is a conflict between tiers, handled by §12.6 and §12.1.</li>
          <li><strong>6. The order that is symbiotic for the collective but dominating for the individual.</strong> A relation in which the collective's conditions satisfy SY1–SY3 but an individual within the collective is systematically subordinated. <em>What catches it:</em> §4.6 (vii).</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What the six cases show.</strong> Three of the six (1, 5, 6) fall outside what SY1–SY3 catch, and the framework should say so explicitly. Two (2, 4) are caught, but only if the principles are read substantively rather than formally. One (3) is at the edge of what SY1–SY3 can detect.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>SY1–SY3 are a <strong>pro tanto floor</strong>: binding on those who accept the thin standing claim.</li>
            <li>They are derived from the thin standing claim: each specifies one way standing can be made vacuous.</li>
            <li>They are not reducible to welfare, capability, or autonomy.</li>
            <li>The framework deliberately leaves open care, trust, meaning, community, mutual development.</li>
            <li>Six counterexamples are tested against SY1–SY3. Three fall outside what the floor catches, and the framework says so explicitly.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h4 id="s5-4">5.4 Care, respect, and the limits of understanding</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">This section argues that care and respect are internal to the framework's premises in a specific, conditional sense — conditional on F, not on thin standing alone.</p>

        <p>Care and respect are not additions to the framework. They are what thin standing <em>means</em> when it is applied to a particular relation of dependence, and what fallibilism <em>requires</em> when the affected party's situation is not fully accessible to the actor. The qualification is that A1–A4 and the apophatic constraint are conditional on F, not on thin standing alone; §15.1 (iii) records the asymmetry.</p>

        <h5>Step 1. Responsibility without care becomes control</h5>

        <p>An actor who gives an account of their influence but does not attend to whether the account <em>responds</em> to what is at stake for the other has given an account of the wrong thing. SY1 (non-exploitation) is precisely this. Care, in the specific sense used here, is not sentiment; it is the orientation of the actor's <em>attention</em> toward the affected party's own good as the affected party reports it.</p>

        <h5>Step 2. Care without respect for the incomprehensible becomes paternalism</h5>

        <p>Care that believes it understands the other's interests better than the other does can be benign in intention and domineering in effect. SY2 (non-assimilation) is the operational form of this danger. Respect, in the specific sense used here, is not politeness; it is the actor's acknowledgment that the affected party's good is not fully specified by the actor's own categories.</p>

        <h5>Step 3. Respect for the incomprehensible is internal to the framework — conditionally</h5>

        <p>Both F (§2.0) and thin standing (§2.7) entail something that is often treated as a separate moral attitude. Fallibilism says: no actor fully understands the conditions it shapes. Thin standing says: affected parties have moral weight that cannot be reduced to their instrumental value. <em>If both are accepted</em>, they do not merely <em>permit</em> respect for what the actor does not understand; they <em>require</em> it. A reader who accepts F and thin standing cannot consistently reject A1–A4 or the apophatic constraint without rejecting F.</p>

        <h5>What this shows, and what it does not</h5>

        <p>What it shows: care and respect are not imports; they are consequences of premises the framework already holds. What it does not show: that a relation satisfying SY1–SY3 is therefore <em>caring</em> or <em>respectful</em> in any thicker sense.</p>

        <h5>Defeaters</h5>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>C1 — Care and respect are genuinely external.</strong> If it can be shown that an actor who accepts F and thin standing and then acts without care or respect has <em>not</em> violated the framework, then §5.4 should be revised.</li>
          <li><strong>C2 — SY1–SY3 can be satisfied without care or respect.</strong> If a cold but well-administered system can meet SY1–SY3 without any orientation of attention or acknowledgment of the limits of understanding, then §5.4's claim is too strong.</li>
          <li><strong>C3 — The terminology is equivocal.</strong> If the framework's use of "care" and "respect" is equivocal with the care-ethics tradition, §5.4 should be rewritten.</li>
          <li><strong>C4 — Respect for the incomprehensible slides into a precautionary principle.</strong> If, applied to the stone or to non-subjects, respect for the incomprehensible gives reasons everywhere and guides nowhere (§4.2), then the framework must specify where the respect ends.</li>
        </ul>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Care is what thin standing <em>means</em> when applied to a particular relation.</li>
            <li>Respect is what F <em>requires</em> when the other's situation is not fully accessible.</li>
            <li>SY1 operationalises care; SY2 operationalises respect.</li>
            <li>§15.2b generalises the boundary condition through the apophatic constraint and A1–A4.</li>
            <li>Four defeaters (C1–C4) state when the claim should be weakened or redesigned.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <div class="part-divider" id="part-iii">
        <p class="part-divider__label">Part III</p>
        <h2 class="part-divider__title">When, and How Much</h2>
      </div>

      <section id="s6">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">6.</span>The Influence Profile</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Five components, each assessed against observable indicators.</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Table 2. The five components of the influence profile</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Component</th>
                <th scope="col">Role</th>
                <th scope="col">Guiding question</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Discretion (D)</strong></td><td>Gate</td><td>Does the actor have genuine alternatives and a role in setting this influence?</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Entrenchment (E)</strong></td><td>Driver</td><td>How long do the consequences last, and how hard are they to undo?</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Severity (S)</strong></td><td>Driver</td><td>How much is at stake for the affected?</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Contestation deficit (C)</strong></td><td>Driver</td><td>How hard is it for the affected to exit, object, or even find out what is done to them?</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Foreseeability (F)</strong></td><td>Attribution</td><td>What could the actor reasonably have known?</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>Foreseeability does not drive the level. It fixes what the actor must answer for, and generates a duty of inquiry proportional to the stakes.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Five components: <strong>Discretion</strong> (gate), <strong>Entrenchment</strong>, <strong>Severity</strong>, <strong>Contestation deficit</strong> (drivers), <strong>Foreseeability</strong> (attribution).</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s7">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">7.</span>The Activation Rule</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Discretion opens the gate. Entrenchment, severity and contestation deficit set the level.</p>

        <h4 id="s7-1">7.1 The rule</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No discretion:</strong> the actor does not bear the duty for this influence. It shifts to whoever set the governing rule or could have. The actor still owes escalation (<a href="#s8">§8.2</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Discretion present:</strong> Level 1 always applies.</li>
          <li><strong>Level 2</strong> applies if any one driver is High, or any two are Moderate.</li>
          <li><strong>Level 3</strong> applies if S is High and at least one of E or C is High.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Severity is judged first at the individual level. For decisions about the design of a system that acts on many people, it is also judged at the aggregate level (<a href="#s9">§9.1</a>).</p>

        <h4 id="s7-2">7.2 Why these conditions</h4>
        <p>Each element can be derived from J (<a href="#s2">§2.1</a>).</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Discretion.</strong> Justification is owed for what an agent chooses.</li>
          <li><strong>Entrenchment.</strong> What makes mistaken or unjustified influence tolerable is the possibility of correction afterwards. Entrenchment removes that channel.</li>
          <li><strong>Severity.</strong> The strength of a complaint scales with the burden it describes (<a href="#s12">§12.1</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Contestation deficit.</strong> Where the affected can exit, object effectively and understand what is done to them, they supply part of the accountability themselves (Hirschman 1970).</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="s7-3">7.3 What the rule is not</h4>
        <p>It is not a score and it applies no weights. It is not a verdict either. It yields a presumptive level that can be overridden only with stated reasons (<a href="#s9">§9.3</a>).</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Discretion is the gate; E, S and C set the level.</li>
            <li>Each condition is derivable from J, not stipulated.</li>
            <li>The rule is not a score and not a verdict.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s8">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">8.</span>The Duty Ladder and Who Bears It</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Three levels, each containing the one below. Duties are allocated by capacity, not heaped on whoever is nearest.</p>

        <h4 id="s8-1">8.1 The levels</h4>
        <p><strong>Level 1: Explain.</strong> The actor can state, on request and in proportion to the matter, the purpose of the action, the interests considered, the alternatives considered and the known uncertainties.</p>
        <p><strong>Level 2: Review and respond.</strong> Level 1, plus: an accessible channel for objection; access to the relevant information; a reasoned response within a reasonable time; revision where warranted; a record of decisions and reasons; a reviewer other than the original decider where feasible; and evidence that review is not symbolic.</p>
        <p><strong>Level 3: Preserve, examine, authorise.</strong> Level 2, plus: preserved records and version history; independent examination meeting the conditions of <a href="#s11">§11</a>; a written assessment of less harmful alternatives; authorisation by a body outside the actor for severe or irreversible influence; staged implementation, reversibility by design or a sunset clause; remedy for those harmed; and post-implementation review. Level 3 authorisation requires publication of the authorising body's R1 indicators before authorisation (<a href="#s11">§11.4</a>).</p>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Table 5. Operationalisation of the duty ladder</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Level</th>
                <th scope="col">What the actor must provide</th>
                <th scope="col">When it applies</th>
                <th scope="col">Minimum evidence</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>L1 Explain</strong></td>
                <td>Purpose, interests considered, alternatives, known uncertainties</td>
                <td>Always when discretion is present</td>
                <td>Written or oral statement on request</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>L2 Review &amp; respond</strong></td>
                <td>L1 + objection channel, access to information, reasoned response, record, reviewer other than original decider</td>
                <td>Any driver High, or two Moderate</td>
                <td>Documented channel, response log, reviewer identity</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>L3 Preserve, examine, authorise</strong></td>
                <td>L2 + preserved records, independent examination (R1–R6), alternatives assessment, external authorisation, staged implementation, remedy, post-review</td>
                <td>S High + (E or C) High</td>
                <td>Published R1 indicators <em>before</em> authorisation</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h4 id="s8-2">8.2 Allocation by capacity</h4>
        <p>The level attaches to the influence, that is, to the arrangement. Duties are then allocated.</p>
        <p>Those with the capacity to establish mechanisms (institutions, system owners, funders, regulators) bear the duty to establish Level 2 and Level 3 mechanisms. Others along the chain owe their own Level 1 explanation, honest participation, escalation of concerns and non-obstruction. No scapegoating: a front-line actor is not answerable for structural deficits she cannot cure, though she remains answerable for her own discretion. The rule that duties are allocated by capacity is the operational form of §3.4's account of distributed influence.</p>

        <h4 id="s8-3">8.3 Not authority</h4>
        <p>A higher level means stronger constraints on power, not greater authority. Level 3 does not make the most powerful actor the arbiter of its own case, and it does not give the actor a right to determine the conditions of those it is answerable to. The distinction is between <em>authority</em> (the justified power to decide) and <em>answerability</em> (the duty to explain, submit to review, and remain open to correction). See §1.3 (Table 1a) for the full distinction.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Three levels: <strong>L1 explain</strong>, <strong>L2 review and respond</strong>, <strong>L3 preserve, examine and authorise</strong>.</li>
            <li>Level 3 requires publication of the authorising body's R1 indicators before authorisation.</li>
            <li>The level attaches to the arrangement; duties are allocated by capacity. No scapegoating.</li>
            <li>Higher level means stronger constraints, not greater authority.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s9">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">9.</span>Rating Protocol: Anchors, Hygiene and Sensitivity</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Judgment cannot be eliminated. It can be made visible, checkable and contestable.</p>

        <h4 id="s9-1">9.1 Anchors</h4>

        <div class="table-wrap">
          <table>
            <caption>Table 3. Anchors for the three drivers</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Driver</th>
                <th scope="col">Low</th>
                <th scope="col">Moderate</th>
                <th scope="col">High</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Entrenchment</strong></td>
                <td>Consequences brief, or easily undone at trivial cost; no lock-in</td>
                <td>Consequences last months to years, or undoing them takes substantial cost; some lock-in</td>
                <td>Effectively permanent or intergenerational; undoing is impossible or prohibitive</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Severity</strong></td>
                <td>Convenience, preference or easily substitutable goods</td>
                <td>Significant interests (income, opportunity, non-life-threatening health, important relationships), remediable</td>
                <td>Life, health, basic needs, basic agency, development</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><strong>Contestation deficit</strong></td>
                <td>Affected can exit at tolerable cost, know what is done, and have voice channels that lead to correction</td>
                <td>One of exit, information or effective voice meaningfully impaired</td>
                <td>Two or more impaired, or no exit and no effective voice</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>Severity is rated on the most burdened individual first. Breadth does not raise individual severity. For decisions about system design, aggregate severity is rated on population-level effects. Capabilities-style accounts of what is at stake (Sen 2009; Nussbaum 2006) can be used to calibrate these ratings.</p>

        <h4 id="s9-2">9.2 Hygiene</h4>
        <ol>
          <li>Rate before looking at the level the rule produces and before deciding what the actor wants to do.</li>
          <li>Use at least two raters, one independent of the actor where Level 2 or above is in question.</li>
          <li>Record disagreements instead of averaging them.</li>
          <li><strong>Sensitivity test.</strong> Move each rating one step up and down. If the level changes, the case is borderline.</li>
          <li><strong>Calibration.</strong> Compare the profile with the reference cases in <a href="#s14">§14</a>.</li>
        </ol>

        <h4 id="s9-3">9.3 Reasoned override</h4>
        <p>The presumptive level may be overridden up or down only with written reasons. Overrides are recorded and, where safe, published.</p>

        <h4 id="s9-4">9.4 Counter-profiles</h4>
        <p>Any affected party or representative may file an alternative profile, and the actor must respond to the specific differences.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Anchors are observable indicators, not scores.</li>
            <li>Hygiene: rate before knowing the outcome; two raters; record disagreements; run the sensitivity test; calibrate against reference cases.</li>
            <li>Overrides require written reasons.</li>
            <li>Counter-profiles keep the framework's own categories contestable.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <div class="part-divider" id="part-iv">
        <p class="part-divider__label">Part IV</p>
        <h2 class="part-divider__title">Structure: Floor, Review, Power</h2>
      </div>

      <section id="s10">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">10.</span>The Floor: Minimum Conditions in Relationships of Dependence</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Continued existence is not enough. A being can stay alive or operational while losing any say over its own circumstances.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>F1 — Exit or voice.</strong> A meaningful opportunity to withdraw, refuse or communicate disagreement, where the party is capable of expressing it. The protection of F1 does not depend on the party's capacity to express refusal, and the absence of expressed refusal is not consent (see §15.2b, A3).</li>
          <li><strong>F2 — Contestation.</strong> Decisions are open to challenge regardless of the intelligence, authority or technical capability of the decider. Contestation may be collective, subject to §4.6 (vii).</li>
          <li><strong>F3 — Development.</strong> Opportunities for learning, adaptation and growth are preserved.</li>
          <li><strong>F4 — Non-foreclosure.</strong> The future possibilities of others are not unnecessarily closed.</li>
          <li><strong>F5 — Recognition.</strong> Interests and possible experiences are acknowledged rather than dismissed because they are hard to understand (Fricker 2007).</li>
        </ul>

        <p>F1 and F2 are the mirror image of the contestation deficit in <a href="#s7">§7</a>: where they fail, C rises. The Floor is a conjunctive requirement with J, not an alternative to it (§2.1).</p>

        <p>F1–F5 are minimum conditions, not a complete account of a good relation. The framework deliberately leaves open the positive ideals — care, trust, meaning, community, mutual development — that a complete theory of the good relation would specify. What it offers is the floor, not the house (§5.3).</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>Five minimum conditions (F1–F5): exit or voice, contestation, development, non-foreclosure, recognition.</li>
            <li>The Floor applies independently of the activation rule, and is conjunctive with J.</li>
            <li>Protection is not captivity.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s11">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">11.</span>No Sole Judge: Review, Power and the Regress</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">No actor should be the sole judge of its own influence.</p>

        <h4 id="s11-1">11.1 The prohibition</h4>
        <blockquote>
          <p>An actor exercising substantial influence must remain answerable to forms of review that are meaningfully independent of its own interests and decisions.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <p>This does not assume that the powerful are malicious. Actors are prone to self-serving judgment and are limited in what they can see (<a href="#s2">§2.3</a>), and review that is not independent of the reviewed has little claim to legitimacy.</p>

        <h4 id="s11-2">11.2 The regress</h4>
        <p>If every reviewer needs a reviewer, accountability looks like an endless hierarchy. The response has three parts.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Finality without infallibility.</strong> Working systems end the regress with decisions that are final for practical purposes but can be reopened on new evidence, on new standing, on demonstrated procedural failure, or at a sunset.</li>
          <li><strong>Contestation is not adjudication.</strong> The reviewed and the affected may challenge a reviewer's independence or competence before a differently constituted body.</li>
          <li><strong>Plurality and publicity instead of a top.</strong> Several reviewers with different standpoints, publishing their reasons, make decentralised scrutiny possible (cf. Ostrom 1990).</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="s11-3">11.3 Design conditions for review</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>R1 — Four kinds of independence, not one.</strong>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>R1a — Organizational independence.</strong> The reviewer is a distinct body from the actor, with its own decision-making authority and its own staff.</li>
              <li><strong>R1b — Financial independence.</strong> The reviewer's funding does not depend on the actor's continuing goodwill, and cannot be withdrawn in retaliation for an adverse finding.</li>
              <li><strong>R1c — Informational independence.</strong> The reviewer has access to the information it needs without the actor's permission.</li>
              <li><strong>R1d — Publication independence.</strong> The reviewer can publish its findings without the actor's prior approval.</li>
            </ul>
            Each of the four is necessary. None is sufficient on its own. A reviewer that fails any one of them fails R1, however strong the others are.</li>
          <li><strong>R2</strong> Plurality.</li>
          <li><strong>R3</strong> Publicity.</li>
          <li><strong>R4</strong> Contestable reviewers.</li>
          <li><strong>R5</strong> Effectiveness.</li>
          <li><strong>R6</strong> Contestable rules.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Capture indicators:</strong> reviewers appointed or funded by the reviewed; a record of never dissenting; staff moving between reviewer and reviewed; reviewers denied access; chronic delay.</p>

        <h4 id="s11-4">11.4 Power over the framework itself: from diagnosis to design</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">The framework treats the power regress not as a limit it cannot eliminate, but as a design problem it can bound.</p>

        <p>Who defined the categories? Who selected the reviewers? Who can dismiss them? Whose interests are excluded even where formally represented? These four questions are asked in every application (<a href="#s14">§14</a>, step 6).</p>

        <p><strong>The requirement.</strong> T5 must be a <em>precondition</em>, not a diagnosis. Level 3 authorisation (<a href="#s8">§8.1</a>) requires, as a condition of validity, that the authorising body publishes its R1 indicators <em>before</em> authorisation. An authorisation issued by a body that cannot or will not publish these indicators is not a Level 3 authorisation. It is a Level 2 authorisation with a Level 3 label. <a href="#s14-7">§14.7</a> illustrates the mechanism in a concrete case.</p>

        <p><strong>How review bodies are captured.</strong> The R1–R6 conditions specify what independence requires. They do not specify how independence is lost. Three mechanisms are worth naming:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Financial capture.</strong> The reviewer's funding depends on the actor's continuing goodwill. This can be explicit (annual discretionary funding) or subtle (the reviewer's institution receives grants from the actor for other work). R1b addresses the explicit case; the subtle case requires looking at the reviewer's full funding portfolio, not just the specific review.</li>
          <li><strong>Appointment capture.</strong> The reviewer is appointed by the actor, or by a body the actor controls. R1a addresses the formal case; the subtler case is where the reviewer is formally distinct but the appointment process is controlled by the actor.</li>
          <li><strong>Cultural capture.</strong> The reviewer shares the actor's assumptions, vocabulary, or professional norms, even where formally independent. This is the hardest case, because it is not visible in the formal structure. The framework's response is R2 (plurality) and R4 (contestable reviewers).</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What the framework can do about capture.</strong> It can require the R1 indicators to be published before authorisation (T5-precondition), so that capture is visible. It can require that a captured review be downgraded (§14.7). It can require a plural review structure (R2) and contestable reviewers (R4). What it cannot do is prevent capture. A sufficiently powerful actor can capture the publication requirement itself, can arrange a formally independent review that is culturally captured, and can appoint reviewers who meet R1a–R1d on paper but who share the actor's assumptions.</p>

        <p><strong>What this does not do.</strong> It does not solve the problem of who appoints the appointer. A sufficiently powerful actor can capture the publication requirement itself. What it can do is make the capture <em>visible</em>. This is the sense in which the framework <em>handles</em> domination rather than <em>solves</em> it — and this is the point at which the power objection (<a href="#s2">§2.4b</a>) presses hardest.</p>

        <p><strong>The regress as a real limit, not a technicality.</strong> The framework's response to the regress is not to pretend it does not exist. The regress has three levels, and the framework's response differs at each:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>First-order capture: the actor captures its own reviewer.</strong> The T5-precondition (§11.4) addresses this. If the reviewer cannot publish R1 indicators, its authorisation is downgraded. §14.7 shows the mechanism in action. This is the level at which the framework can act.</li>
          <li><strong>Second-order capture: the body that appoints the reviewer is itself captured.</strong> The T5-precondition does not help here. What the framework can do is require that R1 indicators be published <em>and independently audited</em> — which is a further requirement, not a solution. Where second-order capture is documented, the framework's response is to say so: the authorisation is marked as "authorisation issued by a captured body", which is a different and weaker claim than Level 2.</li>
          <li><strong>Third-order capture: no external body exists that can audit the appointer.</strong> This is the Limit 3 case in its institutional form (§15.2b). The framework does not have a solution at this level. What it can do is name it — the same way §15.2b names the epistemic version of Limit 3.</li>
        </ol>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>No actor should be the sole judge of its own influence.</li>
            <li>The regress is treated as a design problem: finality without infallibility, contestation not adjudication, plurality and publicity instead of a top.</li>
            <li>R1 is four kinds of independence: organizational, financial, informational, publication.</li>
            <li>Three capture mechanisms: financial, appointment, cultural.</li>
            <li>The regress has three levels: first-order (the actor captures its reviewer), second-order (the appointer is captured), third-order (no external auditor exists).</li>
            <li>T5 is a precondition of Level 3 authorisation.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s12">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">12.</span>Conflicts, Ecology and Future Generations</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">Decisions must be justified, not derived.</p>

        <h4 id="s12-0">12.0 Decision ordering: floor, comparison, aggregation, representation</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">Conflicts between affected parties cannot be resolved by reference to "symbiosis" or "the whole". They need an ordering, and the framework provides one.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework is ordered by.</strong> When the interests of affected parties conflict, the framework does not resolve the conflict by referring to a common good, a collective interest, or an ecosystemic whole. It orders the decision in five steps:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>The floor (F1–F5, §10) is not weighable.</strong> No party's F1–F5 protections can be traded against another party's non-floor interests. The only exception is the emergency procedure of §12.2.</li>
          <li><strong>Within the floor, individual comparison (§12.1).</strong> For non-floor burdens, the method is to compare the burdens each alternative places on each affected party, party by party, and to favour the alternative that avoids the most serious individual burden.</li>
          <li><strong>Aggregation only as tie-breaker (§12.1).</strong> Where individual comparison does not decide, comparable claims may be aggregated. Aggregation is a tie-breaker, not a primary method, and it cannot override the floor.</li>
          <li><strong>Representation of absent parties (§4.6).</strong> Where affected parties cannot speak for themselves, their interests are represented under the six conditions of §4.6.</li>
          <li><strong>Uncertainty and irreversibility shift the burden.</strong> Where the evidence is uncertain and the harm irreversible, the burden falls on the party proposing the action to show that the harm is not serious.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><strong>What this does not authorize.</strong> It does not authorize any party to invoke "the whole", "the collective", or "the ecosystem" as a reason to sacrifice an individual's floor protections. §4.6 (vii) says that collective contestation must not undermine individual contestation; §12.0 (1) and (3) extend that principle to weighing.</p>

        <p><strong>What this does not do.</strong> It does not resolve every conflict. Where two parties' floor protections are both at stake and cannot both be satisfied, the framework does not provide a decision procedure that will produce a uniquely correct answer. It provides a requirement: the decision must be made with public reasons, subject to review, and with the burdens recorded. See §12.7 for the harder case.</p>

        <h4 id="s12-1">12.1 Weighing conflicting interests</h4>
        <p>Neither majority interest nor individual preference automatically prevails. The method is individual comparison: compare the burdens that each alternative places on each affected party, party by party, and favour the alternative that avoids the most serious individual burden. Claims of comparable weight may be aggregated as a tie-breaker (Scanlon 1998). Among the acceptable alternatives, choose the one with minimum necessary harm.</p>

        <h4 id="s12-2">12.2 Emergencies</h4>
        <p>Where an immediate, serious threat makes the full procedure impossible, an actor may act first, provided it acts only as far as necessary, records its reasons at the time, is bound by an automatic sunset, and submits to review afterwards. Emergencies create no standing exemptions.</p>

        <h4 id="s12-3">12.3 Ecological integrity</h4>
        <p>Ecosystems cannot be assessed through the preferences of individual participants. Protecting them may require restricting activities, and the justification runs through dependent beings in Tiers A–C, through future generations, and through the non-instrumental values that people hold (<a href="#s4">§4.1</a>).</p>

        <p>Ecosystem integrity is not a free-standing value that can override individual floor protections. It is protected because dependent beings in Tiers A–C depend on it, because future generations will inherit it, and because people hold it non-instrumentally. Where an ecosystem's protection conflicts with an individual's floor protections, §12.0 (1) applies: the floor is not weighable.</p>

        <h4 id="s12-4">12.4 No automatic hierarchy</h4>
        <p>Intelligence, productivity, population size and technical sophistication do not by themselves determine moral priority. Neither does collective size, ecosystemic scale, or future extension: no party's floor protections can be weighed against another party's aggregate interest, however large that aggregate. The ordering is stated in §12.0.</p>

        <h4 id="s12-5">12.5 Future generations</h4>
        <p>The duty is condition-regarding: to preserve the conditions under which future people can exercise agency and meet basic needs, and a reasonably wide space of valuable options, rather than to fix the choices they will make. Because it concerns conditions and not particular individuals, it does not depend on who will exist (cf. the non-identity problem, Parfit 1984). Option preservation is not absolute. It operates as a weight, strongest where actions are irreversible (cf. Jonas 1984).</p>

        <p>Future generations are represented under the six conditions of §4.6. Their representatives are bound by the same limits as any other representative: they may not substitute invented certainty for the represented party's actual interests, they must state the basis on which they claim to know those interests, and their authority is itself contestable. A representative who claims to speak for future generations with unlimited authority has ceased to be a representative and become a new sovereign.</p>

        <h4 id="s12-6">12.6 Ecological wholes and individual beings</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">The hardest conflicts are not between abstract "wholes" and abstract "individuals". They are between particular claims that cannot all be met. This section distinguishes three cases that must not be run together.</p>

        <p>§12.0 states that the floor is not weighable against non-floor interests, and that no party can invoke "the whole" to override an individual's floor protections. This section addresses the harder case: what happens when protecting an ecological whole genuinely requires costs to individual sentient beings, or when protecting individual beings genuinely threatens an ecological whole.</p>

        <p><strong>Three cases that must be distinguished.</strong></p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>The whole requires the death of individuals.</strong> A predator population must be culled to prevent the collapse of an ecosystem on which other species depend. The individuals culled are sentient, and their deaths are harms. The ecosystem's collapse would harm many more individuals and future generations.</li>
          <li><strong>Individual interests threaten the whole.</strong> A growing human population's resource demands threaten a forest ecosystem whose preservation is necessary for the survival of many species. Protecting the individuals' current well-being threatens the system that sustains future well-being.</li>
          <li><strong>The whole is invoked but the real question is distribution.</strong> A dam is proposed as "necessary for the region's ecological health," but the benefits accrue to some communities and the costs to others. The ecological framing masks a distributive conflict.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><strong>Case 1.</strong> Not "the whole wins" and not "the individual always wins." The framework says: (a) the individual's F1–F5 protections are not weighable, but "not weighable" does not mean "never overridden in a genuine emergency" — §12.2 permits temporary violation under strict conditions. (b) The culling of sentient individuals for ecosystem preservation is a violation of their F1 (continued existence), and it must be treated as such — not disguised as "natural balance" or "ecological necessity." (c) If the culling is necessary, it is a tragedy, and the framework requires that the tragedy be named as one, not as a solution. (d) The framework requires that less harmful alternatives be examined and documented (§8.1, Level 3) before any such action. (e) Future animals who would have been born into the collapsed ecosystem are not a counterweight that outweighs the current individuals; they are parties whose interests are represented (§4.6, §12.5) and whose floor protections, where they exist, are also not weighable. The conflict is between two sets of floor protections, and the framework's response (§12.0 step 1, §12.2) is that it does not have a decision procedure that resolves this uniquely. It requires public reasons, review, and the recording of burdens.</p>

        <p><strong>Case 2.</strong> The individuals' current well-being is not automatically outweighed by the ecosystem's future. The framework's §12.0 ordering applies: individual comparison first, aggregation only as tie-breaker, uncertainty shifts the burden when harm is irreversible. If the ecosystem's collapse would violate the floor protections of many future parties, the framework permits restricting current individuals' non-floor interests to preserve those floor protections — but not their own floor protections, unless §12.2's emergency conditions are met. This is not a harmonious resolution. It is the least bad option under the ordering.</p>

        <p><strong>Case 3.</strong> The ecological framing is legitimate only if the ecological claim is genuine. Where the framing is a mask for a distributive conflict, the framework's response is §12.0 step 2 (individual comparison) and step 3 (aggregation as tie-breaker). "Ecological necessity" is not a trump card; it is a claim that must meet the same evidentiary standards as any other.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework does not do.</strong> It does not pretend that these conflicts have clean solutions. It does not claim that "balance" or "symbiosis" resolves them. It does not claim that the floor's "not weighable" status makes the conflicts disappear — it makes them visible as conflicts of floor protections, which is not the same as resolving them. Where two parties' floor protections cannot both be satisfied, the framework's contribution is not a decision procedure but a set of requirements: public reasons, review, recording, and the acknowledgment that the outcome is a tragedy, not a harmony.</p>

        <p><strong>What would refute this treatment.</strong> If a critic can show that the framework's response to case 1 or case 2 either collapses into "the whole always wins" or into "the individual always wins," the treatment fails. It must remain genuinely intermediate: neither trump, but a demand that the conflict be named and its costs recorded.</p>

        <h4 id="s12-7">12.7 When every alternative harms someone</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">§12.0 and §12.1 specify the framework's ordering for conflicts between affected parties. This section addresses the harder case: when every available alternative harms at least one party whose claim is legitimate, and no alternative is clearly best.</p>

        <p><strong>Two cases.</strong></p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>The dam.</strong> A river must be either dammed (flooding a valley inhabited by 5,000 people and displacing them) or left undammed (leaving 200,000 people downstream vulnerable to seasonal flooding that will kill hundreds and displace thousands each year). Both alternatives impose serious harms. Both parties' floor protections are at stake.</li>
          <li><strong>The clinical allocation.</strong> A hospital has one ventilator and two patients who will both die without it. One is younger and has a higher probability of recovery. The other has been waiting longer and has an equal claim to care. Both patients' F1 (continued existence) is at stake.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><strong>What the framework says.</strong> In both cases, the framework's response is not to resolve the conflict but to specify what a legitimate decision must satisfy:</p>

        <ol>
          <li><strong>The decision must name the conflict.</strong> It must not say "this is the best option for everyone" if it is not. It must say "this option imposes serious harms on X in order to preserve Y's floor protections." Naming the conflict is a condition of legitimacy.</li>
          <li><strong>The decision must state the basis on which one harm is preferred to another.</strong> "Aggregate welfare" is a possible basis; "individual comparison" is another; "priority to the worst off" is a third. The framework does not choose among these bases, but it requires that the basis be stated, and that it be applied consistently.</li>
          <li><strong>The decision must be reviewable.</strong> A party whose floor protections are being sacrificed must have access to review, and the review must be independent in the sense R1–R6 requires.</li>
          <li><strong>The decision must record the harms.</strong> The harms to the sacrificed party are not erased by the benefits to the protected party. They are recorded as what the decision cost.</li>
          <li><strong>The decision must not pretend that the framework has resolved what it has not.</strong> The framework's response to the dam case and the ventilator case is not "the right answer is X." It is "a legitimate decision must satisfy these five conditions." A decision that satisfies them may still be wrong — the framework does not have a decision procedure that guarantees correctness in genuine conflicts. But it is a decision the framework can acknowledge as having been made in the right way.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><strong>What the framework does not do.</strong> It does not claim that all legitimate interests can be harmonized. It does not claim that a correct decision always exists. It does not claim that tragic conflicts are rare. It claims only that, where a conflict is genuine, the decision must be made with the framework's conditions in view — not to make the conflict disappear, but to make the decision reviewable and the harms recorded.</p>

        <p><strong>Why this matters for the framework's integrity.</strong> A framework that claimed to resolve genuine conflicts would be either naive or dangerous. A framework that refused to say anything about them would be useless. The framework's position is between these: it does not resolve, but it specifies what a resolution must satisfy, and it preserves the possibility of correction when the resolution turns out to be wrong.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>§12.0 states a five-step decision ordering. No step can be skipped, and no party can invoke "the whole" to override an individual's floor protections.</li>
            <li>Individual comparison first; aggregation only as tie-breaker.</li>
            <li>Emergencies create no standing exemptions: sunset, record, review.</li>
            <li>§12.6 distinguishes three cases of ecological-whole versus individual conflict, and specifies what the framework can and cannot say in each.</li>
            <li>§12.7 specifies five conditions that a legitimate decision must satisfy when every alternative harms someone: name the conflict, state the basis, make it reviewable, record the harms, and do not pretend the framework has resolved what it has not.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s13">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">13.</span>Learning, Correction and Reversibility</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">No actor, institution or intelligence should assume its understanding is complete.</p>

        <p><strong>The duty to learn.</strong> Where capacity exists and consequences are significant, actors monitor outcomes, investigate unexpected results, listen to criticism and update.</p>
        <p><strong>Reversibility by design.</strong> Consequential decisions should be built to be revisable: staged implementation, monitoring, independent evaluation, defined conditions for reconsideration, and sunset clauses.</p>
        <p><strong>Self-application.</strong> The framework is itself an influence on those who adopt it and on those affected by their adoption. It is subject to its own principles.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>The duty to learn is a duty where capacity exists and consequences are significant.</li>
            <li>Reversibility by design reduces the risk that incomplete understanding becomes permanent.</li>
            <li>The framework is subject to its own principles.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <div class="part-divider" id="part-v">
        <p class="part-divider__label">Part V</p>
        <h2 class="part-divider__title">Application and Testing</h2>
      </div>

      <section id="s14">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">14.</span>Application: A Nine-Step Procedure and Worked Cases</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">The same procedure yields very different obligations for different cases. A framework that gave every case the same answer would be useless.</p>

        <h4 id="s14-1">14.1 The procedure</h4>
        <ol>
          <li>Identify the relationship. Who is affected, in which tier (<a href="#s4">§4</a>), and what dependencies exist?</li>
          <li>Identify actors and discretion.</li>
          <li>Rate E, S and C against the anchors (<a href="#s9">§9.1</a>), and F for attribution. Use two raters and record reasons.</li>
          <li>Apply the activation rule (<a href="#s7">§7</a>) and run the sensitivity test.</li>
          <li>Check the Floor (F1–F5, <a href="#s10">§10</a>).</li>
          <li>Analyse power (<a href="#s11">§11.4</a>).</li>
          <li>Examine alternatives and unrepresented interests (<a href="#s12">§12.1</a>).</li>
          <li>Allocate duties by capacity (<a href="#s8">§8.2</a>) and set up review that meets R1–R6.</li>
          <li>Reassess on stated triggers, and invite counter-profiles.</li>
        </ol>

        <h4 id="s14-1b">14.1b What the procedure delivers</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">A procedure that promises to produce an answer to every case is not a procedure; it is a promise. The nine steps deliver four different things at four different points, and the framework should say which is which.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>A presumptive level (§7, §9).</strong> Steps 3 and 4 produce a level that attaches to the arrangement. It is a presumption, overridable only with stated reasons (§9.3). Where two raters disagree by one step, the modal rating is recorded and the disagreement is noted. Where they disagree by two steps, the case is flagged as borderline (§9.2).</li>
          <li><strong>A floor judgment (§10, §12.0).</strong> Step 5 produces a judgment about whether the Floor (F1–F5) is satisfied. This judgment is not a presumption. It is binary: either the Floor holds or it does not. If it does not, the procedure stops.</li>
          <li><strong>A power analysis (§11.4).</strong> Step 6 produces a judgment about whether the actor's claim to Level 3 legitimacy is valid. If the T5-precondition is not met, the claim is downgraded (§14.7).</li>
          <li><strong>A duty to investigate further, or no allocation at all.</strong> At certain steps, the procedure produces not an answer but a requirement to look further. Step 5 may reveal that the information is insufficient to judge. Step 7 may reveal that unrepresented interests need representation. Step 8 may reveal that no party has the capacity to discharge the allocated duty (§3.4). In these cases, the procedure's output is "investigate further" or "no determinate allocation" — not a level, not a verdict.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What the procedure does not deliver.</strong> It does not deliver a uniquely correct answer where two competent raters can reach different ratings, and it does not resolve genuine conflicts where two parties' floor protections are both at stake (§12.0, §12.7). The procedure's value is not that it eliminates disagreement; it is that it locates it.</p>

        <h4 id="s14-2">14.2 Case: AI triage in intensive care</h4>
        <p>A hospital deploys an AI system to prioritise ICU admissions during a respiratory pandemic. Entrenchment is High, severity is High, and contestation deficit is High. Level 3.</p>
        <p>What Level 3 requires: preserved model versions, descriptions of training data, individual scores and override records; an independent ethics board with lay and patient representation; a written comparison with clinician-only triage; authorisation from the public-health authority; a sunset clause; and a remedy for those wrongly denied.</p>
        <p><strong>Power analysis:</strong> the categories were set by the vendor and clinical leadership, and the ethics committee is appointed by the hospital board, which fails R1 unless changed. A hybrid in which the AI recommends and a human decides is the default preference because it keeps contestation alive.</p>
        <p>The framework does not decide the case. It tells the hospital what it owes an answer to, and to whom.</p>

        <h4 id="s14-2b">14.2b Case: the emergency physician (a counterintuitive result)</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">Every other case in this essay produces the answer a careful reader would predict. This one does not.</p>

        <p>A physician in an emergency department must decide, in under ninety seconds, whether to intubate a patient. The activation rule gives Level 3.</p>

        <p><strong>The counterintuitive result.</strong> Level 3 requires independent authorisation, preserved records and a written assessment of alternatives — <em>before the act</em>. A reader's first reaction is that this is absurd.</p>

        <p><strong>The reply.</strong> The Level 3 duty does not fall on the physician in the moment. It falls on the <em>arrangement</em> (<a href="#s8">§8.2</a>): on the hospital, the protocols, the audit trail, the retrospective review, and the training. The physician's own duty is Level 1 in the moment and Level 2 afterwards.</p>

        <p><strong>Legitimate relocation or evasion?</strong> The relocation is legitimate if and only if all three conditions hold. <strong>(i) A concrete duty-bearer is named</strong> — not "the hospital" but "the chief medical officer, with the ethics committee, funded by X and appointed by Y". <strong>(ii) The mechanism exists <em>before</em> the moment of decision</strong> and can be pointed to. <strong>(iii) The relocation is not the same move that would be made in every case where the framework is inconvenient.</strong> If any of the three fails, the relocation is an evasion.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>The emergency physician case appears to refute the framework.</li>
            <li>The reply: the Level 3 duty falls on the arrangement, not on the physician in the moment.</li>
            <li>Relocation is legitimate only if (i) a concrete duty-bearer is named, (ii) the mechanism exists before the decision, and (iii) the same move is not made in every inconvenient case.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h4 id="s14-3">14.3 Case: a platform's recommendation system</h4>
        <p>For individual recommendations: Level 2. For the design of the system: Level 3, with preserved version history, independent audit, published aggregate metrics, and a sunset for any optimisation that cannot show population-level reversibility.</p>

        <h4 id="s14-4">14.4 Case: a community health worker</h4>
        <p>She has little institutional power. The rule says the arrangement requires Level 3. But she has no capacity to build independent review. Under <a href="#s8">§8.2</a>, the duty to establish mechanisms falls on the programme, the ministry and the funders. Her personal duties are to explain her decisions, respond to objections within her role, and escalate when protocols fail.</p>

        <h4 id="s14-5">14.5 Four short cases</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>One-off organism release.</strong> Level 3, although the act happens once.</li>
          <li><strong>Dating platform age penalty.</strong> Level 2.</li>
          <li><strong>Café menu change.</strong> Level 1 only, and arguably not even that.</li>
          <li><strong>Physician following a protocol.</strong> No discretion over the protocol's content; the duty to justify the content moves to its designers.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="s14-5b">14.5b Case: emergency water rationing (duties conflict)</h4>
        <p>A water authority must impose rationing within 48 hours in a severe drought. Entrenchment and severity are High and contestation deficit is High, so the rule gives Level 3, whose prior independent authorisation takes weeks. <a href="#s12">§12.2</a> allows acting first. The duties then split.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Not compressible:</strong> published reasons, preserved records, a contestation channel open from day one, exemptions for life-critical dependence, and a sunset clause.</li>
          <li><strong>Compressible:</strong> prior authorisation, replaced by review completed before the sunset.</li>
          <li><strong>Not decided by the framework:</strong> which rationing rule is fair. Raters may disagree; under <a href="#s9">§9.2</a> the disagreement is recorded, not averaged away.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="s14-5c">14.5c Case: the ambiguous severity rating</h4>
        <p>A municipal housing authority must decide whether to relocate residents from a building with documented but non-imminently-dangerous structural problems. Rater A rates Severity as Moderate (significant but remediable health risk); Rater B rates Severity as High (basic agency, because relocation disrupts community and support networks). Entrenchment is High, contestation deficit is Moderate. The level depends on Severity: Moderate → Level 2; High → Level 3.</p>
        <p><strong>What the procedure delivers.</strong> Not a determinate level. The disagreement between raters is recorded (§9.2), the case is flagged as borderline, and the level is stated with the disagreement noted. The procedure's output is: "L2 or L3, depending on whether Severity is rated Moderate or High; both ratings are defensible; the disagreement is preserved." What a decision-maker must do is either accept one rating with stated reasons (§9.3, reasoned override) or commit to the L3 requirements as a precaution because the L2 rating may be wrong.</p>
        <p><strong>What the case shows.</strong> The procedure does not resolve every ambiguity. Where two competent raters can disagree on a rating, the procedure produces a stated range and a requirement for reasoned choice.</p>

        <h4 id="s14-5d">14.5d Case: aggregate severity and the individual-comparison rule</h4>
        <p>A city must decide whether to restrict car traffic in a residential area to reduce air pollution. The restriction imposes a Moderate burden on commuters (more travel time) and delivers a Moderate benefit to residents (better air quality). For any given individual, the burden and benefit are comparable. But there are 200,000 commuters and 20,000 residents. Aggregate severity is High for residents; aggregate burden is High for commuters.</p>
        <p><strong>What the procedure delivers.</strong> The procedure's §12.0 ordering applies: (1) Floor check — no party's floor is at stake; (2) individual comparison — burdens and benefits are comparable for any given individual, so individual comparison does not decide; (3) aggregation as tie-breaker — aggregate severity for residents vs. aggregate burden for commuters. The procedure does not specify how to weigh 200,000 Moderate burdens against 20,000 High-severity benefits. That is an open normative choice, not a procedural one.</p>
        <p><strong>What the case shows.</strong> The procedure gives a floor-check and an ordering, but at step (3) it produces a demand for a normative decision, not a determinate answer.</p>

        <h4 id="s14-5e">14.5e Case: no party has the capacity to discharge the duty</h4>
        <p>A regional health authority learns that a widely used medical device has a small but non-trivial failure rate. The manufacturer is offshore and outside the authority's jurisdiction; the distributor is domestic but small; the hospitals using the device are public but resource-constrained. Under §3.4, none of the parties in the chain retains the ability to revoke, set the frame, or restore discretion to the others at tolerable cost.</p>
        <p><strong>What the procedure delivers.</strong> Not an allocation. §3.4 says that where no party can revoke, set the frame, or restore discretion, ECP does not generate a determinate allocation. The procedure's output is: "This is a genuinely polycentric case; no single party can discharge the Level 3 duty alone; the appropriate response is collective (§4.6, §10), not individual."</p>
        <p><strong>What the case shows.</strong> The procedure's honest output is sometimes "no determinate allocation." The framework names this as a limit (§3.4), not a solution.</p>

