        <h4 id="s14-7">14.7 Case: an AI company's internal audit (T5-precondition failing)</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">This case shows what happens when the framework's T5-precondition is not met: the actor does not lose the framework; the actor loses the claim to Level 3 legitimacy.</p>

        <p>An AI company commissions an "independent" audit of its recommendation system, which has been shown to amplify polarising content. The audit is published with a foreword by the CEO, the reviewers are paid by the company, and the company's board appoints them. The audit concludes that the system is "broadly safe" and recommends "continued monitoring."</p>

        <p><strong>The framework's assessment.</strong> The company has conducted a review. But the review fails R1 (independent appointment, funding and removal) and would fail T5 as a precondition (<a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a>). Under the framework, this means the review does not count as a Level 3 authorisation. It is a Level 2 authorisation with a Level 3 label.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework tells the regulator.</strong> The regulator does not accept the audit as satisfying Level 3. The regulator asks for: R1 indicators; a second, genuinely independent review, funded by the regulator, not the company; and a published R1 statement, as a condition of any future Level 3 claim.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework tells the company.</strong> The company does not lose the framework. It loses the claim to have met Level 3. It can still meet Level 2 by providing access to information and responding to objections. But it cannot claim the stronger legitimacy that comes with a genuinely independent review.</p>

        <h4 id="s14-7b">14.7b Case: the captured regulator (second-order capture)</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">§14.7 shows the first-order case: a company captures its own auditor. This case shows the second-order case: the body that would audit the company is itself captured. It is the harder case, and the framework's response is correspondingly weaker.</p>

        <p>A national data protection authority is asked to approve an AI company's recommendation system under the framework's Level 3 requirements. The authority's director was appointed by the same minister whose party received donations from the company's parent. The authority's budget is set annually by the ministry. The authority's technical staff have moved between the authority and the company over the past five years. The authority publishes its R1 indicators — but they reveal exactly this pattern.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework's T5-precondition delivers.</strong> The precondition is met formally. But the indicators themselves reveal that R1a is partially met, R1b is not met, R1c is partially met, and R1d is met formally but the authority's publications are widely understood to be constrained by the ministry's preferences.</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework says.</strong> The authorisation does not count as a Level 3 authorisation. The framework's response is to mark it as <em>"authorisation issued by a captured body"</em> — which is weaker than Level 2, because Level 2 requires an actual reviewer, and a captured body is not a reviewer in the framework's sense (§11.3).</p>

        <p><strong>What the framework cannot do.</strong> It cannot tell the company what to do next. It can tell the company that its authorisation is not a Level 3 authorisation. It can tell the public that the authority is captured. But if the authority is captured by the same power that captured the company, and no court is available that is not itself captured, then the framework's response is exhausted. This is the third-order case (§11.4), and the framework does not solve it.</p>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>A captured review does not count as Level 3 authorisation.</li>
            <li>A captured regulator does not issue a Level 3 authorisation, even if it publishes its R1 indicators.</li>
            <li>"Authorisation issued by a captured body" is weaker than Level 2.</li>
            <li>§14.7 shows the framework's strongest move; §14.7b shows it failing.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>
      </section>

      <section id="s15">
        <h3 class="section-title"><span class="num">15.</span>Limits, Testing and Replacement</h3>
        <p class="section-lede">This section states what the framework's ground does not rest on, the three structural limits the framework cannot close, and what follows for its status as a diagnostic.</p>

        <h4 id="s15-1">15.1 The ground is not derived</h4>
        <p>The framework rests on three premises it does not derive. Saying so is not a failure. Saying otherwise is.</p>

        <p><strong>(i) B is a premise, not a theorem.</strong> §2.0 states B as a bridge norm. F motivates B. F does not entail B. Therefore B is a premise.</p>

        <p><strong>(ii) Thin standing is a premise, not a theorem.</strong> §2.7 states thin standing as the framework's first normative commitment. It is chosen, not derived.</p>

        <p><strong>(iii) The operational components stand on their own — with one exception.</strong> The activation rule, the duty ladder, the rating protocol, the T5-precondition and the SY1–SY3 indicators do not depend on whether B or thin standing are correct. The exception is the apophatic constraint and A1–A4, which are conditional on F (§15.2b, §5.4). This separation is the framework's main protection against the charge that it presupposes the morality it tries to derive.</p>

