        <h4 id="s15-2">15.2 Three structural limits</h4>
        <p>These are not limitations of execution. They are limitations of what any procedure of the kind the framework proposes can see.</p>

        <p><strong>Limit 1 — Harmonious Domination.</strong> A society that satisfies every rule and every indicator, but the terms the dependent party accepts are terms it would reject at tolerable exit cost <em>if it could imagine the alternative</em>. The framework cannot detect this, because the framework's indicators are measured on conditions the dominant party has set. The framework's partial response is a change of default: testimony from a party inside a structurally asymmetric relation is not evidence <em>for</em> symbiosis unless independent confirmation exists. This is not enough.</p>

        <p><strong>Limit 2 — Non-formulable harm.</strong> A harm that the harmed party cannot formulate — because the resources for formulating it have been withheld, or because the party's form of life is not legible in the framework's terms — falls outside the indicators. The framework's partial response is to treat silence as a presumption against, not a neutral condition.</p>

        <p><strong>Limit 3 — Unreadable dominance.</strong> A sufficiently advanced intelligence, or a sufficiently well-organised institution, can satisfy every procedural requirement while controlling a party whose form of life it does not understand and cannot make legible. The framework's partial response is to add understanding as a condition for L3. But this response has its own limit. The dominant party may believe it understands — and be wrong, in ways its own categories cannot detect. And in the Limit 3 case, where no party outside the superior can verify compliance, this specification has no binding force at all — a limit that §15.2b states plainly.</p>

        <h4 id="s15-2b">15.2b How to act toward what you cannot understand</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">The three limits in §15.2 are stated negatively: they say what the framework cannot see. The question this section addresses is positive: what can a superior intelligence do, given those limits? The answer is not a procedure, and the section says so.</p>

        <p><strong>The question.</strong> Can a superior intelligence preserve another being's existence, freedom and developmental possibilities without understanding what that being really is — and without making its own understanding the measure?</p>

        <p><strong>The honest answer.</strong> The superior cannot <em>certify</em> that it has avoided making its own understanding the measure. Every act of preservation is an act of classification, and the classification is the superior's. There is no general procedure that guarantees non-imposition. But the claim that imposition is <em>unavoidable in every respect</em> is too strong, and the framework should not make it. The categories can be thin, revisable and shadowed by alternatives; the superior can be required to keep them open to correction.</p>

        <div class="key-prop" data-label="The apophatic constraint">
          <p>The superior must preserve not the being's current self-understanding (which may be captive, or simply not the being's own), and not the superior's understanding of the being (which is the measure we are trying to avoid), but the <strong>structural conditions under which the being can develop and express a self-understanding that the superior cannot anticipate</strong>. The superior must act <em>as if</em> the being's reality exceeds its understanding, and must build that assumption into its action.</p>
        </div>

        <p><strong>Three epistemic gaps, not one.</strong> The apophatic constraint addresses a situation that is often described as a single gap but is actually three distinct gaps, and each calls for a different response:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The experiential gap.</strong> The superior has no access to what the being's experience is like — or, where the being may not have experience in the relevant sense, no access to whether it does. Response: humility and precaution, but not paralysis — the gap does not tell the superior what to do, only that its own certainty about the being's inner states is limited.</li>
          <li><strong>The descriptive gap.</strong> The superior cannot describe the being's states in terms the being would recognize as accurate. Response: A2 (do not require justification in the superior's terms) and the funding of translation and intermediaries.</li>
          <li><strong>The causal gap.</strong> The superior cannot know whether its actions help or harm the being, because the being's responses are unreadable or the causal pathways are too complex. Response: A3 (do not read non-resistance as consent) and A4 (treat resistance as evidence against the superior's understanding), plus structural indicators that do not depend on reading the being's inner states.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>Four conditions follow.</strong> They are not procedures. They are structural commitments that constrain how the superior acts, without telling it what to do.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>A1 — Preserve the capacity to resist.</strong> The superior must preserve the conditions under which the being can express refusal — refusal that the superior cannot anticipate, in a form the superior may not recognize as refusal.</li>
          <li><strong>A2 — Do not require justification in the superior's terms.</strong> The superior must not require the being to justify its refusal in the superior's categories.</li>
          <li><strong>A3 — Do not interpret non-resistance as consent.</strong> The superior must not treat the being's apparent acceptance as evidence that the being is flourishing.</li>
          <li><strong>A4 — Treat resistance as evidence against the superior's understanding.</strong> When the being resists, the superior must treat this as evidence that its understanding is inadequate — not as evidence that the being is defective.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>How the conditions guide action.</strong> A1–A4 are not procedures, but each has an operational test.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>A1 in practice.</strong> Identify at least one channel the party has used to refuse in the past; confirm it is currently open; if no such channel is known, create one (e.g. an unmonitored channel) and treat its absence as evidence of capture.</li>
          <li><strong>A2 in practice.</strong> When the party contests, the superior must not demand that the contestation be reformulated in the superior's terms.</li>
          <li><strong>A3 in practice.</strong> Do not treat absence of refusal as evidence of acceptance. Default to "unknown" and seek structural confirmation (SY1, §5.1).</li>
          <li><strong>A4 in practice.</strong> When resistance occurs, the superior's first response is inquiry, not correction.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>How A1–A4 can fail in practice.</strong> Five failure modes deserve naming: formal channel capture (A1), translation burden (A2), paralysis by A3, resistance misread as noise (A4), and institutional absence (all four).</p>

        <p><strong>When the requirements collide.</strong> Humility, precaution, reversibility, and preservation of agency are not always compatible. The framework does not resolve these collisions by a principle. It resolves them by a procedure: where the requirements collide, the collision must be named, the reasons for the chosen balance must be documented, and the choice must be reviewable.</p>

        <p><strong>Whether A1–A4 follow from the premises.</strong> They follow from F and B, but only in a specific sense. From F and B, what follows is: the superior must not treat its own understanding as sufficient warrant for its action, and it must seek correction from parties not controlled by itself. From this, A1–A4 are reasonable specifications of what "not treating its own understanding as sufficient" means in practice. But the specification is not unique. A1–A4 are a <strong>normative commitment</strong> (§2.0a) rather than a logical consequence. A reader who accepts F and B but rejects A1–A4 has not made a logical error; they have made a different normative choice.</p>

        <p><strong>What could reduce the Limit 3 problem without solving it.</strong> Five mechanisms are worth naming, each with its limits:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Self-limitation.</strong> A superior that publicly commits to A1–A4 and submits to review by a party it could have avoided creates a check that would not otherwise exist.</li>
          <li><strong>Voluntary binding.</strong> A superior that has built its legitimacy on A1–A4, and would lose that legitimacy by breaking them, has an incentive to comply even where no external enforcement exists.</li>
          <li><strong>Technical control.</strong> A system designed to preserve the being's capacity to resist (an unmonitored channel, a log the superior cannot alter) is a technical control.</li>
          <li><strong>Plural review.</strong> Where genuinely independent reviewers exist (R1–R6, §11.3), the Limit 3 case is not present. But plural review can itself be captured (§11.4, §14.7b).</li>
          <li><strong>Preserved possibility of future correction.</strong> A superior that preserves records and uncertainty statements makes it possible for a later party to examine what it did. This is not verification at the time; it is verification later.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>None of these solves the problem. All of them can reduce it.</p>

        <p><strong>The Limit 3 problem for A1–A4.</strong> In the Limit 3 case — a superior intelligence that exceeds every possible verifier — <em>there is no party outside the superior that can verify compliance</em>. A1–A4 then have no binding force in the sense of enforceable obligations. The framework must say this plainly. The claim is not that A1–A4 cease to be obligations in the Limit 3 case. It is that they cease to be <em>enforceable</em> obligations. A moral obligation can exist without an enforcement mechanism; that is the standard position of the justificatory tradition the framework inhabits (§2.7).</p>

        <p><strong>What remains in the Limit 3 case.</strong> Even where A1–A4 cannot be enforced, four things remain possible: the knowledge claims can be made visible; uncertainty can be documented; the possibility of later correction can be preserved; and power concentration can be named.</p>

        <p><strong>Identifying resistance in a party you do not understand.</strong> Four operational indicators suffice: (i) any repeatable deviation from the superior's expectation that the party could in principle use to signal; (ii) refusal to engage in the expected form; (iii) use of a channel the superior did not authorize; (iv) a change in the party's behaviour correlated with the superior's action.</p>

        <p><strong>Breaking the heads-I-win problem.</strong> A3 says acceptance is not evidence; A4 says resistance is evidence against the superior. Taken alone, these two make it impossible for any relation to count as confirmed. The framework's response is to deny that either attitude <em>toward the current arrangement</em> is the appropriate evidential source. Independent confirmation of legitimacy is a structural fact.</p>

        <p><strong>What counts as independent confirmation.</strong> Four sources, none of them a reading of the party's attitude toward the superior: (i) SY1 under non-coercive elicitation; (ii) exercised refusal in low-stakes contexts; (iii) structural indicators — dissent channels exist and have been used, the vocabulary to formulate objections is available, intermediaries exist that are not controlled by the superior; (iv) counterfactual improvement. The primary structural confirmations are sources (iii) and (iv), not (i). SY1 remains a necessary indicator, but it is the weakest of the four.</p>

        <p><strong>Does A2 conflict with J?</strong> No. J requires that justification be <em>open to contestation</em> — a property of the arrangement, not of the party. A2 forbids the superior to <em>require</em> the party to justify itself in the superior's terms.</p>

        <p><strong>Is this paternalism?</strong> Not in the specific sense. Paternalism involves acting on the other's behalf because the superior believes it knows what is good for the other. The four conditions explicitly prohibit this belief.</p>

        <p><strong>What would refute this answer.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>If a superior intelligence can demonstrate that it has acted toward a being it does not understand without imposing its own categories, the apophatic constraint would be unnecessary.</li>
          <li>If the four conditions systematically fail to preserve what they claim to preserve, they should be revised or abandoned.</li>
          <li>If the four conditions can be satisfied by a sufficiently powerful actor without any change in the actor's underlying disposition, they are not a real constraint.</li>
        </ul>

        <aside class="key-points">
          <ul>
            <li>The apophatic constraint addresses three distinct gaps (experiential, descriptive, causal), each calling for a different response.</li>
            <li>Four conditions: A1 (preserve the capacity to resist), A2 (do not require justification in the superior's terms), A3 (do not interpret non-resistance as consent), A4 (treat resistance as evidence against the superior's understanding).</li>
            <li>A1–A4 are a <strong>normative commitment</strong>, not a logical consequence. The specification is not unique.</li>
            <li>When the requirements collide, the collision must be named, documented, and reviewable.</li>
            <li>Five mechanisms can reduce the Limit 3 problem without solving it: self-limitation, voluntary binding, technical control, plural review, preserved possibility of future correction.</li>
            <li>In the Limit 3 case, A1–A4 cannot be enforced, though they remain obligations. Four things remain possible: making knowledge claims visible, documenting uncertainty, preserving the possibility of later correction, and naming power concentration.</li>
            <li>The heads-I-win problem is broken by structural confirmation, not by SY1 alone: sources (iii) and (iv) carry more confirmatory weight than SY1.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <h4 id="s15-3">15.3 What the framework is, therefore</h4>
        <p><strong>The framework is not a procedure for producing legitimacy. It is a diagnostic for locating where legitimacy is likely to fail.</strong></p>

        <p>This distinction matters, because it changes what the framework is asked to do. A procedure that can be followed and still fail is a failed procedure. A diagnostic that identifies failures before they happen is a working diagnostic. The framework is the second. Its job is not to certify that a relation is legitimate. Its job is to identify the places where procedure will pass while something valuable is still being lost — and to make those places visible, contestable and, where possible, correctable.</p>

        <p><strong>What would count as the framework being wrong.</strong> Four outcomes would count as refutation rather than application. <strong>(i) Worse outcomes on independent measures.</strong> If, over a preregistered multi-year comparison, adopters produce systematically worse outcomes for the most burdened than matched non-adopters on independently measured harm indicators, the diagnostic is wrong. <strong>(ii) Miscalibrated floor.</strong> If a relation that the diagnostic rates as failing SY1–SY3 reliably predicts what independent observers judge to be a good relation, the choice of floor is wrong. <strong>(iii) Superseded categories.</strong> If a rival diagnostic identifies the same failures as this one without the three structural limits, this framework is superseded, not applied. <strong>(iv) T5 backfires.</strong> If the T5-precondition is shown to <em>increase</em> capture by giving captured reviewers a legitimacy label they did not previously have, the framework's central defence against the power objection has failed.</p>

        <h4 id="s15-4">15.4 What the framework can be tested against</h4>
        <p>The diagnostic has its own success conditions. They are modest, but they are conditions.</p>

        <p><strong>Preregistration.</strong> Before any test is run, the calibration set of cases, the raters, the outcome measures, the thresholds and the analytic plan must be fixed and made public.</p>

        <p><strong>The tests are proposed, not performed.</strong> The framework has not been empirically validated. The calibration table in §14.6 contains the author's own ratings. They are not the product of an independent panel, they have not been compared with the ratings of independent experts, and no pilot study has been conducted.</p>

        <p><strong>Five failure conditions, stated as commitments.</strong></p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>T1 Reliability.</strong> The anchors do not produce agreement among trained raters on a calibration set of diverse cases. If this cannot be corrected after two revisions, the diagnostic is not usable as a decision aid.</li>
          <li><strong>T2 Discriminative validity.</strong> The levels fail to track the safeguards that independent expert panels, blind to the diagnostic's output, judge necessary.</li>
          <li><strong>T3 Effectiveness.</strong> In matched or staggered-adoption comparisons, adopting institutions fail to improve on independent measures of decision quality, correction rate, or affected-party trust.</li>
          <li><strong>T4 Harm.</strong> Adopters show worse outcomes for the most vulnerable groups than matched controls on independently measured harm indicators.</li>
          <li><strong>T5 Capture and legitimation.</strong> A majority of adopters' review bodies fail R1 on documented appointment, funding, and removal, or the rate of successful external challenges falls without improvement on the T3 indicators.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What operationalisation can and cannot do.</strong> The framework's operational machinery can identify patterns. It cannot, by itself, determine what is morally right. Three risks deserve explicit statement:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The measurability bias.</strong> Indicators tend to privilege what is easy to measure over what is important. A framework that measures contestation deficit by counting formal complaint channels will miss the cases where channels exist but are unusable. The framework's response is to specify structural indicators (iii and iv in §15.2b) that do not depend on counting formal channels, and to name the limits of the indicators (§15.2, Limit 2).</li>
          <li><strong>The precision illusion.</strong> A rating of "Moderate" on severity looks precise, but it is a judgment, not a measurement. §9.2 (hygiene) and §14.5c (ambiguous severity case) are explicit about this.</li>
          <li><strong>The validation gap.</strong> A pilot that passes T1 (reliability) shows that raters agree. It does not show that raters agree on the morally important thing.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><strong>What qualitative testimony can do that measurement cannot.</strong> A framework that relies only on indicators will miss harms that the harmed party cannot formulate in the indicators' terms. The framework's response is to require qualitative testimony (§5.2) alongside quantitative indicators, to treat non-formulable harm as a structural limit (§15.2, Limit 2), and to treat silence as a presumption against, not a neutral condition.</p>

        <p><strong>The philosophical claim, separated from the empirical one.</strong> The framework's philosophical claims — that J is necessary but not sufficient, that normative symbiosis is a pro tanto floor, that A1–A4 are conditional on F — do not depend on the pilot succeeding. What the pilot tests is reliability, not validity. The validity questions are stated in <a href="#appendix-a">Appendix A, §A.7b</a>.</p>

        <h4 id="s15-5">15.5 Replacement</h4>
        <p>The diagnostic is modular. A diagnostic D′ should replace the current one if:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>D′ improves on at least one independent indicator in §15.4 or resolves one of the structural limits in §15.2 or §15.2b.</li>
          <li>D′ causes no unacknowledged regression elsewhere.</li>
          <li>D′ is operationally applicable and produces more defensible identifications of failure on a substantial set of cases.</li>
          <li>D′ is corrigible: it states its own failure conditions.</li>
          <li>The transition is trialled, with a sunset or a route back.</li>
          <li>The incumbent is not privileged.</li>
        </ol>

        <h4 id="s15-6">15.6 Consolidated limits and defeasibility</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">This section collects, in one place, the claims the framework does <em>not</em> make and the conditions under which its parts should be rejected.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>What the framework does not claim.</strong> (i) That J is necessary as a deduction; (ii) that satisfying J is sufficient for a non-arbitrary relation; (iii) that B or thin standing are derived; (iv) that the operational components all depend on F, or that none do (see §15.1, Table 7); (v) that a relation meeting SY1–SY3 is therefore good; (vi) that the framework solves domination; (vii) that procedure certifies legitimacy; (viii) that the three structural limits can be closed from inside; (ix) that the superior can certify that it has avoided imposing categories; (x) that A1–A4 are self-enforcing; (xi) that A1–A4 are enforceable in the Limit 3 case — they are not; (xii) that the activation rule has been empirically validated; (xiii) that the empirical tests have been performed — they have not; (xiv) that the selection of SY1–SY3 is the only possible floor; (xv) that SY1–SY3 are fully independent of one another; (xvi) that SY1 is independent confirmation on its own; (xvii) that ECP generates a determinate allocation in every distributed case; (xviii) that meeting SY1–SY3 and F1–F5 is sufficient for a good relation; (xix) that the framework can reliably distinguish a party that has genuinely accepted its conditions from a party whose capacity to reject them has been dismantled; (xx) that the framework resolves every conflict between affected parties; (xxi) that the nine-step procedure produces a uniquely correct answer in every case; (xxii) that the framework's review mechanisms are immune to capture; (xxiii) that answerability is the same as blame, responsibility, authority, or sovereignty.</li>
          <li><strong>Additional non-claim (from §2.0a):</strong> The framework does not derive its normative claims from its descriptive claims. Universal symbiosis does not entail normative symbiosis. The move requires additional premises (P1–P5), stated openly in §2.0a.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4 id="s15-7">15.7 Operational falsification conditions</h4>
        <p class="section-lede">§15.6 collects the framework's defeaters by section reference. This section makes them operational.</p>

        <p><strong>Three levels of failure.</strong> A framework can fail at three different levels, and the response differs at each: Level 1 (application failure), Level 2 (empirical hypothesis failure), Level 3 (philosophical premise failure).</p>

