      <section id="objections">
        <h2>Common objections</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Fifteen objections the framework has to survive. Each is a real objection; none is fatal. Each objection states what would change if it held.</p>

        <p class="expand-row">
          <button class="btn" id="expandObjections" aria-expanded="false">Expand all objections</button>
        </p>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">1.</span> "Why must the powerful justify themselves to the affected? What if they simply refuse?"</summary>
          <p class="reply">This is the hardest objection, and the framework does not pretend to refute it. The reply has four parts. First, the framework is <em>internal to the thin justificatory tradition</em> (<a href="#s2-7">§2.7</a>), and a reader who rejects even the thin standing claim is outside the framework's scope. Second, the framework does not attempt a performative refutation of the realist. Third, the framework makes realist capture visible and costly. Fourth, and most importantly, the epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5">§5.2</a>) makes the point sharper: the powerful party cannot <em>know</em> whether the relation is symbiotic without the affected party's contestation. The refusal to justify is not just a moral failing; it is an epistemic one.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If the realist is right that thin standing cannot be justified, the framework's scope shrinks to those who accept thin standing. This is not a small change — it converts the framework's claimed breadth (§2.7) into a much narrower scope.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">2.</span> "This is a values framework dressed up as a procedure."</summary>
          <p class="reply">Partly, and the essay says so (<a href="#s2">§2.6</a>, <a href="#s2-7">§2.7</a>, <a href="#s15">§15.1</a>). J carries a minimal commitment to the equal standing of those affected. A fair test is whether people with different comprehensive views can use it to reach different answers in the same case. They can: it does not decide the ICU case.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If the framework's procedures cannot be separated from its substantive values, then §15.1's claim that the operational components stand on their own is false. The procedural machinery would need to be reformulated as a thick normative theory, not a diagnostic.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">3.</span> "Proceduralism is itself a form of power."</summary>
          <p class="reply">This is the second-hardest objection, treated at length in <a href="#s2">§2.4b</a> and <a href="#s15">§15.2</a>–<a href="#s15">15.3</a>. The framework does not deny it. Its reply has three parts: (i) the objection is partly correct, and the framework's T5-as-precondition is its internal response (<a href="#s14-7">§14.7</a> shows it in action); (ii) the objection is not specific to proceduralism; (iii) the framework's modesty is not an admission of failure.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If the objection holds in full, the T5-precondition fails as an internal defence, and the framework's claim to "handle" domination becomes "cannot even handle it." §16's concession would need to be weakened further.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">4.</span> "Why should contestation override better outcomes?"</summary>
          <p class="reply">It does not always. Where exit and review are available and effective, justification need not be owed (<a href="#s2">§2.1</a>). The framework's J is disjunctive, not monistic. The remaining disagreement is genuine, and the framework sides with contestation in severe cases for epistemic reasons, while permitting temporary override in emergencies subject to sunset and review (<a href="#s12">§12.2</a>).</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If contestation should not be privileged even where exit and review fail, J's disjunctive structure is wrong. The epistemic burden principle would need to be reformulated as "one consideration among many."</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">5.</span> "The capacity principle legitimises power."</summary>
          <p class="reply">Answerability is not authority (§1.3, Table 1a; §8.3). Higher levels mean stronger constraints. T5 looks for exactly this misuse. The risk remains and is not argued away; <a href="#s11">§11.4</a> converts it from a concession into a design constraint.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If capacity-based allocation predictably legitimates power, §8.2 needs to be abandoned or supplemented. The emergency physician case (§14.2b) would not be a legitimate relocation but an evasion.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">6.</span> "Who reviews the reviewers?"</summary>
          <p class="reply">The regress is bounded, not eliminated (<a href="#s11">§11</a>): finality without infallibility, review conditions R1–R6, plural and public reasoning, and counter-profiles. It also needs a political precondition, a balance of power, that no framework can supply by itself. See <a href="#s11-4">§11.4</a> for the three levels of capture.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If the regress cannot be bounded, the review mechanisms are circular. R1–R6 would need to be reformulated to require a stopping point that is not itself a review.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">7.</span> "Precaution under uncertainty is either paralysing or too weak to matter."</summary>
          <p class="reply">The credibility threshold in <a href="#s4">§4.2</a> answers the first: reasons arise only where there is evidence or a plausible mechanism. Against the second: in Tier C the principle does real work, requiring investigation and proportionate safeguards. The stone does not meet the threshold and is not claimed to.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If the plausibility threshold cannot be specified non-vacuously, Tier C cannot do the work it is meant to do. The four tiers would collapse into two.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">8.</span> "The ratings are arbitrary or manipulable."</summary>
          <p class="reply">Judgment cannot be eliminated, but <a href="#s9">§9</a> exposes it: anchors with observable indicators, two raters, a sensitivity test, reasoned overrides and counter-profiles. Tests T1 and T2 examine consistency, and <a href="#s15">§15.4</a> requires preregistration.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If the anchors cannot be made reliable after two revisions (T1), the rating protocol is abandoned. The framework's claim to be a usable diagnostic would not survive.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">9.</span> "This is Western liberal philosophy in disguise."</summary>
          <p class="reply">Its development lies mostly in that tradition, and <a href="#s15">§15.1</a> says so. <a href="#s2-7">§2.7</a> distinguishes thin from thick justificatory claims: the framework requires only the thin one, which is much more widely shared. It engages other traditions (<a href="#s2">§2.5</a>).</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If the thin/thick distinction does not succeed, the framework is a Western liberal framework with universal pretensions — which would not refute it, but would narrow its claim and require a different justification of its breadth.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">10.</span> "It will burden the already overburdened."</summary>
          <p class="reply">The level attaches to the arrangement and duties are allocated by capacity (<a href="#s8">§8.2</a>). The health worker case (<a href="#s14">§14.4</a>) and the emergency physician case (<a href="#s14">§14.2b</a>) show the mechanism. The relocation criterion in §14.2b is what distinguishes a legitimate relocation from an evasion.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If allocation by capacity does not successfully relocate duties, the framework burdens the already-burdened. This would make it regressive in a way its own normative commitments prohibit.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">11.</span> "The epistemic burden principle is circular."</summary>
          <p class="reply">This is one of the most serious objections to the framework's positive contribution. SY1–SY3 are specified independently of answerability. The principle adds a substantive claim: these three conditions can only be <em>reliably assessed</em> through procedures of answerability. This is not tautological. The principle may be wrong — the argument can be challenged, and the defeaters are stated in <a href="#s5">§5.2</a> — but the formulation does not define symbiosis in terms of answerability.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If SY1–SY3 cannot be specified independently of answerability, the epistemic burden principle is a tautology. The framework would lose its distinctive contribution (§1.5).</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">12.</span> "Why not just rely on rights and regulation?"</summary>
          <p class="reply">Rights and regulation are necessary and are not replaced. They leave gaps: influence by actors the law does not reach, structural harms not yet recognised as rights violations, and cases where the law lags.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If rights and regulation cover the cases, the framework is redundant. This would not refute it, but it would narrow its contribution to the cases rights and regulation do not reach.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">13.</span> "Why these three symbiosis indicators, and not something else?"</summary>
          <p class="reply">The choice is derived and defended in <a href="#s5-3">§5.3</a>. SY1–SY3 are the three distinct ways standing can be made vacuous: hollow reasons, absorbed difference, foreclosed action. They are a <em>pro tanto floor</em>, not a complete ideal. If a critic can show that a relation clearly failing SY1–SY3 is nonetheless good, or that a relation clearly meeting them is nonetheless bad in a way the framework cannot detect, the choice of floor should be revised.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If §5.3's derivation fails, the floor is arbitrary. The framework would need a different floor, requiring re-arguing everything from §5 onwards.</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">14.</span> "How can a superior intelligence act toward what it cannot understand without making its own understanding the measure?"</summary>
          <p class="reply">This is the hardest question, and the answer is in <a href="#s15">§15.2b</a>. The honest answer: it cannot certify that it has avoided making its own understanding the measure. What the superior <em>can</em> do is preserve the structural conditions under which the being can express a self-understanding the superior cannot anticipate (A1), refuse to require justification in its own terms (A2), refuse to read non-resistance as consent (A3), and treat resistance as evidence against its own understanding (A4). A1–A4 are stated as <em>normative commitments</em>, not as logical consequences. In the Limit 3 case, A1–A4 cannot be enforced, though they remain obligations.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If A1–A4 cannot be satisfied without imposing the superior's categories, or can be satisfied by a superior with no intention of respecting the being, the apophatic constraint is not a constraint. The framework's response to the hardest question would be "no answer is possible."</p>
        </details>

        <details class="objection">
          <summary><span class="obj-num">15.</span> "Does the framework actually decide anything, or does it always defer?"</summary>
          <p class="reply">This is the objection that the framework's own structure invites. The framework says it is a diagnostic, not a procedure (§15.3). It produces a presumptive level, not a verdict (§7.3, §9.3). It does not resolve genuine conflicts (§12.0, §12.7). It sometimes produces "investigate further" or "no determinate allocation" (§14.1b, §14.5e). A reader could conclude that the framework never actually decides anything.</p>
          <p class="reply">The objection is partly correct. The framework does not decide specific cases. What it does decide is: what questions must be answered, by whom, in what form, and with what review. That is a real decision. A framework that says "the hospital must obtain independent review before implementing the AI triage system" (§14.2) is deciding something. A framework that says "the company's audit does not count as Level 3 authorisation because it fails R1" (§14.7) is deciding something.</p>
          <p class="reply">But the objection has force in one respect: the framework does not decide whether the ICU triage AI is a good idea, whether the recommendation algorithm should be deployed, whether the water rationing is fair. These questions require substantive theories of justice (§2.4, §2.5) that the framework does not provide, and pretending to answer them would exceed what the framework can justify.</p>
          <p class="change"><strong>What this would change:</strong> If the framework's "diagnostic, not procedure" framing is a cover for never deciding anything, the framework is not a framework at all. It is a set of questions, not a theory. The framework's own position (§15.3) is that the questions are the contribution. But a critic who says "questions are not enough" has identified a genuine limitation, not a misunderstanding, and the framework should acknowledge it as one.</p>
        </details>
      </section>

