      <section id="glossary">
        <h2>Glossary</h2>
        <dl class="glossary">
          <dt>Universal symbiosis</dt>
          <dd>The descriptive claim that existence is relationally woven — through dependence, influence, interaction, competition, conflict, destruction and mutual creation. A worldview, not an ethics. Does not entail normative symbiosis (§5, §5.0a, §2.0a).</dd>

          <dt>Normative symbiosis</dt>
          <dd>A pro tanto floor for relations of dependence: the minimum conditions under which dependence is not exploited, difference is not forced into sameness, and the dependent party keeps the conditions for continued existence, development and participation. Specified by SY1–SY3 (§5.1). §5.3 derives the choice of floor from the thin standing claim.</dd>

          <dt>Three kinds of claim</dt>
          <dd>Logical consequences (follow from premises), normative commitments (defended by argument, not deduced), and practical recommendations (operationalise commitments, tested separately). Stated in §2.0a.</dd>

          <dt>The additional premises (P1–P5)</dt>
          <dd>Standing, vulnerability, capacity, fallibility, contestation. Together with the relational premise, they motivate the framework's normative claims without entailing them from the descriptive claim alone (§2.0a).</dd>

          <dt>Fallibilist premise (F) and bridge norm (B)</dt>
          <dd>F: no actor fully understands the conditions it shapes. B: where others bear the cost of an actor's errors and may see what it cannot, the actor should not be sole judge of its influence. B is a premise, not a theorem (§15.1).</dd>

          <dt>Normative location</dt>
          <dd>The framework is internal to the <em>thin</em> justificatory tradition, not the thick one (§2.7). Thin standing is a premise, not a theorem (§15.1). Three considerations support it as a starting point without proving it from nothing (§2.7).</dd>

          <dt>Answerability</dt>
          <dd>The duty to give reasons, permit examination, and remain open to correction. Distinct from blame, responsibility, authority, and sovereignty (§1.3, Table 1a).</dd>

          <dt>J</dt>
          <dd>The core principle: systematic constraint of others' conditions requires at least one of meaningful exit, independent review, or justification open to contestation (§2.1). Primarily epistemic; abductively defensible, not a transcendental deduction. Defeaters stated in §2.2b. Necessary but not sufficient (§2.1).</dd>

          <dt>Apophatic constraint</dt>
          <dd>The requirement that the superior preserve the structural conditions under which the being can develop and express a self-understanding the superior cannot anticipate, and act as if the being's reality exceeds the superior's understanding (§15.2b). Addresses three distinct gaps: experiential, descriptive, causal. Not a procedure; a structural commitment.</dd>

          <dt>Apophatic conditions (A1–A4)</dt>
          <dd>Four structural commitments that constrain how the superior acts toward a being it does not understand (§15.2b): A1 preserve the capacity to resist; A2 do not require justification in the superior's terms; A3 do not interpret non-resistance as consent; A4 treat resistance as evidence against the superior's understanding. Stated as <em>normative commitments</em>, not logical consequences (§2.0a). Five named failure modes. In the Limit 3 case, they remain obligations but cannot be enforced.</dd>

          <dt>Limit 3</dt>
          <dd>The case where no party outside the superior can verify compliance with A1–A4. A1–A4 cease to be enforceable, but they do not cease to be obligations. Four things remain possible: making knowledge claims visible, documenting uncertainty, preserving the possibility of later correction, and naming power concentration (§15.2b).</dd>

          <dt>Three epistemic gaps</dt>
          <dd>Experiential (no access to what the being's experience is like); descriptive (cannot describe the being's states in terms it would recognize as accurate); causal (cannot know whether the superior's actions help or harm). Each calls for a different response (§15.2b).</dd>

          <dt>Heads-I-win problem</dt>
          <dd>The risk that A3 and A4 together make any relation unconfirmable. Broken by refusing to read either attitude as the primary evidential source: independent confirmation is a structural fact, and the structural indicators (iii) and (iv) in §15.2b are the primary instances. SY1 is the weakest of the four.</dd>

          <dt>Three levels of capture</dt>
          <dd>First-order: the actor captures its own reviewer (addressed by T5-precondition; §14.7). Second-order: the body that appoints the reviewer is itself captured ("authorisation issued by a captured body", weaker than Level 2; §14.7b). Third-order: no external auditor exists (§11.4; the institutional form of Limit 3).</dd>

          <dt>Five contributory roles</dt>
          <dd>Causing, enabling, controlling, foreseeing, benefiting. Each maps onto responsibility differently in distributed systems (§3.4).</dd>

          <dt>Three relations within universal symbiosis</dt>
          <dd>Causal influence (A affects B); dependence (A requires B); ecological belonging (A is part of a system). They can come apart (§5.0a).</dd>

          <dt>Local and systemic</dt>
          <dd>Local actions can produce systemic effects, and systemic changes can produce local effects. The framework's activation rule and its account of distributed influence are attempts to locate responsibility in cases where the two cannot be separated cleanly (§5.0a, §3.4).</dd>

          <dt>Ecological wholes vs. individual beings</dt>
          <dd>Three cases distinguished in §12.6: whole requires death of individuals; individual interests threaten whole; whole invoked but real question is distribution. The framework does not resolve these uniquely; it specifies what a legitimate decision must satisfy.</dd>

          <dt>Genuine tragedy</dt>
          <dd>The case where every available alternative harms at least one party whose claim is legitimate (§12.7). The framework does not resolve such conflicts; it specifies five conditions that a legitimate decision must satisfy: name the conflict, state the basis, make it reviewable, record the harms, and do not pretend the framework has resolved what it has not.</dd>

          <dt>Three levels of failure</dt>
          <dd>Application failure (Level 1), empirical hypothesis failure (Level 2), philosophical premise failure (Level 3). Each requires a different kind of revision (§15.7).</dd>

          <dt>Diagnostic (not procedure)</dt>
          <dd>The framework's status (§15.3). It does not certify legitimacy; it locates where procedure will pass while something valuable is still being lost. Four falsifying outcomes stated in §15.3.</dd>

          <dt>Reliability vs. validity</dt>
          <dd>Reliability: agreement among raters (Krippendorff's α). Validity: whether raters agree on the morally important thing. The framework does not claim that passing T1 establishes validity (§15.4, Appendix A §A.7b).</dd>

          <dt>Exploratory vs. confirmatory</dt>
          <dd>Exploratory evidence: whether an instrument is usable at all (a pilot of thirty cases can provide this). Confirmatory evidence: whether it is reliable across the population (requires hundreds of cases, preregistration, replication). The pilot in Appendix A is exploratory (§A.7c).</dd>

          <dt>Operational falsification condition</dt>
          <dd>A stated condition under which a claim would be revised or abandoned, specified at the level of detail the framework commits to. §15.7 (Table 8) contains the operational falsification conditions, grouped by three levels.</dd>

          <dt>The Floor (F1–F5)</dt>
          <dd>Minimum conditions in relationships of dependence: exit or voice, contestation, development, non-foreclosure, recognition (§10). Conjunctive with J, not an alternative. The Floor is a floor, not a house.</dd>

          <dt>Symbiosis indicators (SY1–SY3)</dt>
          <dd>Non-exploitation, non-assimilation, continued possibility (§5.1). Not fully independent, but independent enough to do work that one indicator could not — as three worked examples show. SY1 is the weakest confirmatory source in §15.2b. Indicators are diagnostic, not verdictive.</dd>

          <dt>"Exit at tolerable cost"</dt>
          <dd>The total cost of leaving the relation (material, social, psychological, opportunity) does not violate the party's F1–F5 protections. Where no real alternative exists, SY1 is assessed through structural indicators and counterfactual improvement (§5.1).</dd>

          <dt>Decision ordering</dt>
          <dd>The five-step ordering in §12.0 for conflicts between affected parties. No party can invoke "the whole" to override an individual's floor protections.</dd>

          <dt>Author's ratings (Table 4)</dt>
          <dd>The ratings in §14.6 are the author's own, not a panel's. They have not been independently validated, and no pilot study has been conducted.</dd>
        </dl>
      </section>

