      <section id="discussion">
        <h2>Discussion and contact</h2>
        <p>This essay is offered as a framework to be argued with. Two channels are open for that purpose, and a third — the comment thread below — for anything that fits neither.</p>

        <div class="note">
          <strong>Call for comments.</strong> Seventeen specific areas of criticism are invited in particular:
          <ol style="margin-top:.6rem;margin-bottom:0">
            <li><strong>The universal vs normative symbiosis distinction (<a href="#s2-0a">§2.0a</a>, <a href="#s5">§5</a>)</strong> — is the descriptive/normative distinction clear enough? Does the essay ever slip into deriving norms from the relational premise?</li>
            <li><strong>The specific claim (<a href="#s1-5">§1.5</a>)</strong> — is the interlocking operational set genuinely new, or is it a recombination of existing pieces?</li>
            <li><strong>J's sufficiency (<a href="#s2-1">§2.1</a>)</strong> — is the necessary-but-not-sufficient framing adequate?</li>
            <li><strong>The normative location (<a href="#s2-7">§2.7</a>)</strong> — is the distinction between thick tradition and thin standing claim successful, and are the three positive considerations for thin standing adequate?</li>
            <li><strong>The ground is not derived (<a href="#s15">§15.1</a>)</strong> — is the framework's acknowledgment of B and thin standing as premises sufficient?</li>
            <li><strong>The three structural limits (<a href="#s15">§15.2</a>)</strong> — are the responses to Harmonious Domination, Non-formulable harm, and Unreadable dominance adequate?</li>
            <li><strong>The apophatic constraint (<a href="#s15">§15.2b</a>)</strong> — are the three epistemic gaps distinct enough? Are A1–A4 adequately specified as normative commitments rather than logical consequences? Do the five named failure modes cover the ways A1–A4 can fail? Is the treatment of the Limit 3 case honest enough?</li>
            <li><strong>Ecological wholes vs. individual beings (<a href="#s12-6">§12.6</a>)</strong> — does the three-case treatment genuinely stay intermediate between "the whole always wins" and "the individual always wins"?</li>
            <li><strong>Genuine tragedy (<a href="#s12-7">§12.7</a>)</strong> — are the five conditions that a legitimate decision must satisfy adequate, or do they launder the tragedy?</li>
            <li><strong>The falsifying outcomes (<a href="#s15">§15.3</a>)</strong> — are the four outcomes genuine refutations, or are they restatements of the framework's own predictions?</li>
            <li><strong>The adversarial tests (<a href="#s2-8">§2.8</a>)</strong> — are the replies to Pettit, Forst and Jonas genuinely adversarial?</li>
            <li><strong>The relocation criterion (<a href="#s14-2b">§14.2b</a>)</strong> — does the three-condition test distinguish legitimate relocation from evasion?</li>
            <li><strong>SY1–SY3's independence and SY1's limits (<a href="#s5">§5.1</a>)</strong> — are the three worked examples convincing? Is the weighting of structural indicators (iii)/(iv) above SY1 defensible?</li>
            <li><strong>The choice of floor (<a href="#s5-3">§5.3</a>)</strong> — does the derivation from thin standing succeed?</li>
            <li><strong>The epistemic burden principle (<a href="#s5">§5.2</a>)</strong> — is the argument circular? Are the five defeaters (B1–B5) adequate?</li>
            <li><strong>The calibration table (<a href="#s14-6">§14.6</a>)</strong> — is the panel form adequate, given that the panel is not independent in the sense required for T1?</li>
            <li><strong>The pilot (<a href="#s15-4">§15.4</a>, <a href="#appendix-a">Appendix A</a>)</strong> — is the n=30 sample realistic, are the α thresholds right, is the procedure reproducible?</li>
          </ol>
          <p style="margin-top:.6rem;margin-bottom:0">Substantive objections that are not addressed will be listed in a future revision. The framework is offered as a tool, and its proposed epistemic principle is open to defeat.</p>
