      <section id="appendix-a">
        <h2>Appendix A — Empirical Validation Protocol</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">This appendix specifies the pilot study that would provide evidence relevant to T1 (§15.4). It is placed here, and not in the main text, because the main text's argument is philosophical; the appendix is operational.</p>

        <h3>A.1 What the pilot would test</h3>
        <p>The pilot would test T1 (reliability): whether trained raters, working independently from the same anchors, reach agreement on the level that the activation rule assigns to a diverse set of cases. It would not test T2–T5.</p>

        <h3>A.2 Sample</h3>
        <p><strong>Reference cases.</strong> The twelve reference cases in Table 4 (§14.6).</p>
        <p><strong>Additional cases.</strong> Eighteen additional cases drawn from published regulatory, clinical, and platform-governance decisions, selected by an independent party, not by the framework's author.</p>
        <p><strong>Total.</strong> Thirty cases.</p>

        <p><strong>Why thirty.</strong> The number is not a statistical-power calculation. It is a feasibility number derived from three constraints: (i) three raters × thirty cases = ninety ratings, the minimum workload that can be completed in a single workshop; (ii) thirty cases stratified across three drivers × three levels × three sectors yields roughly three to four cases per stratum; (iii) Krippendorff's alpha requires variation to be meaningful.</p>

        <h3>A.2b Stratification</h3>
        <p>The thirty cases are stratified across four dimensions: driver levels, assigned level, sector, and difficulty (clear-cut vs. borderline). See §A.2b in the full protocol for the table.</p>

        <h3>A.3 Raters</h3>
        <p><strong>Number.</strong> Three raters. <strong>Independence.</strong> At least two of the three must be independent of the framework's author. <strong>Training.</strong> All three trained on the anchors in §9.1, and on a sample of five training cases not in the test set.</p>

        <h3>A.4 Procedure</h3>
        <ol>
          <li>Each rater rates all thirty cases independently, in random order.</li>
          <li>Raters do not see the others' ratings before submitting their own.</li>
          <li>Raters do not see the framework's evaluation outcomes, the author's prior ratings, or the panel ratings in Table 4.</li>
          <li>Disagreements are recorded, not averaged.</li>
          <li>Ratings are submitted before any analysis is conducted.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>A.5 Analysis</h3>
        <p><strong>Primary measure.</strong> Krippendorff's alpha on the assigned level (L1, L2, L3) across all three raters, computed on all thirty cases.</p>
        <p><strong>Decision rule.</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>If α ≥ 0.800, the framework passes T1 at the validated level.</li>
          <li>If 0.667 ≤ α &lt; 0.800, the framework passes T1 at the tentative level.</li>
          <li>If 0.40 ≤ α &lt; 0.667, the anchors are revised and the pilot is repeated.</li>
          <li>If α &lt; 0.40 after two revisions, the diagnostic is abandoned as a decision tool.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>A.6 Why the thresholds are 0.667 and 0.800</h3>
        <p>Krippendorff's own guidance is that α ≥ 0.667 supports tentative conclusions, and α ≥ 0.800 supports reliance on the results (Krippendorff 1980, 2004).</p>

        <h3>A.7 What the pilot would not establish</h3>
        <p>Reliability (T1) is necessary for validity (T2) but not sufficient. A pilot that passes T1 has shown that the anchors are usable; it has not shown that the anchors track what independent expert panels would judge necessary.</p>

        <h3>A.7b What alpha cannot show: the validity problem</h3>
        <p>Krippendorff's alpha measures agreement among raters, not truth. Three validity questions must be kept distinct: construct validity (do the drivers track what they claim to measure?), content validity (do the anchors cover the framework's stated scope?), criterion validity (do the levels predict what independent experts judge necessary?). Alpha cannot detect systematic bias that all raters share.</p>

        <h3>A.7c What a pilot of this size can and cannot establish</h3>
        <p>The pilot is <strong>exploratory</strong>, not <strong>confirmatory</strong>. Exploratory: whether the anchors are usable at all. Confirmatory: whether the anchors are reliable across the population of cases. A pilot with thirty cases can answer the first question. It cannot answer the second.</p>

        <h3>A.8 Reproducibility</h3>
        <p>The case set, training procedure, preregistered analysis plan, raters' identity (to the extent consistent with their consent), raw ratings, and analysis code must be made public before any analysis is conducted.</p>

        <h3>A.9 Institutional arrangements</h3>
        <p>A pilot meeting the conditions above would require: an academic partner or independent research organization, open funding, publication of results regardless of outcome, and an independent steering committee not appointed by the framework's author.</p>

        <h3>A.10 What the framework says about its own empirical status</h3>
        <p>No pilot has been run. The calibration table in §14.6 is the author's, not a panel's. The framework is a diagnostic, not a validated instrument.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="about">
        <h2>About this text</h2>
        <p><em>By Jesper Blom.</em></p>
        <p>This is an independent philosophical essay. It has not been peer reviewed, and it does not claim the authority of an established tradition. It is offered as a framework — a set of distinctions that may be useful for reasoning about responsibility in contexts where capability is growing faster than accountability.</p>
        <p>The most recent revision (November 2026) addresses several long-standing issues: the distinction between universal symbiosis (descriptive) and normative symbiosis (prescriptive), stated openly in §2.0a; the explicit acknowledgement that the framework's normative claims are normative commitments rather than logical consequences of its premises; the three epistemic gaps in the apophatic constraint (§15.2b); the treatment of ecological wholes versus individual beings (§12.6); the treatment of genuine tragedy (§12.7); and Objection 15, which asks whether the framework ever actually decides anything.</p>
        <p><strong>What has not been done.</strong> No pilot has been run. The ratings in Table 4 are the author's, not a panel's. The framework's empirical claims are unconfirmed. The consolidated list of what the framework does not claim is in <a href="#s15-6">§15.6</a>.</p>

        <div class="note"><strong>Corrections and criticism</strong> are welcome. A framework that cannot survive disagreement was never a framework to begin with.</div>

        <p style="font-size:.85rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:2rem">Cite as: Blom, J. <em>The Responsibility of Existence: A Framework for Answerability under Asymmetric Influence</em>. November 2026. the-responsibility-of-existence.github.io</p>
